All courses in the Department of Chicano and Latino Studies are grounded in race, power, and justice. As an ethnic studies interdisciplinary field, this is our foundation. While other disciplines, such as history, English, or sociology, have individual scholars or subfields that are interested in these concepts, our discipline was founded on analyzing the world through this lens. Therefore, ethnic studies fields, such as ours, are leaders within the academy, offering some of the most innovative scholarship that addresses race, power, and justice. Additionally, community engagement is central to the origins and development of Chicano and Latino Studies. Many courses include community-engagement opportunities and support career options.

The minor in Chicano and Latino Studies is designed to meet the needs of students preparing for careers serving Chicano, Chicana, Chicanx, Chicane, Latino, Latina, Latinx, and Latine constituencies and to prepare students for graduate and advanced professional study in programs in which a minority affairs focus would be an asset. The program allows students the flexibility of pursuing work in related fields, such as Latin American studies, Spanish, political science, African American studies, Women's studies, and American studies. ", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramLOs": "<ul><li><p>Can identify, define, and solve problems</p></li><li><p>Can locate and critically evaluate information</p></li><li><p>Have mastered an interdisciplinary body of knowledge and mode of inquiry</p></li><li><p>Understand diverse philosophies and cultures within and across societies</p></li><li><p>Can communicate effectively</p></li><li><p>Understand the role of creativity and innovation and expression across multiple disciplines</p></li><li><p>Have acquired skills for engaged and life-long learning and relational leadership for social justice.</p></li></ul>", "cdProgramMinorType": "Minor related to a major", "cdProgramNameShort": "Chic Stdy", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Undergraduate minor", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cla.umn.edu/chicano-latino" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/chicano-latino</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>Name update to remove the hyphen, which has lead to confusion in the catalog search function.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "reportAsNscProgram": false, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TIOT - College of Science and Engineering", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionDegreeUgrd": "A bachelor's degree in an engineering, basic science, or mathematics program is preferred. ", "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": 3, "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "The application deadlines are December 3 for fall admission and August 31 for spring admission. All materials must be submitted to the online application. ", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLReadScore": "19", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": "79", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLWritScore": "21", "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "30", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The Civil Engineering Master of Science (MS) degree balances education in engineering fundamentals and design. The degree prepares students for careers in industry as well as for continued studies at the doctoral level. The degree offers students the opportunity to focus on one of the following areas of engineering environmental (e.g., pollutant fate and transport, process modeling, soil and groundwater remediation, water and wastewater treatment), geomechanics (e.g., fracture and localization, groundwater flow, stability and liquefaction, wave and shock propagation), structural (e.g., computational and structural mechanics, earthquake engineering, infrastructure performance and durability, new systems and materials), transportation (e.g., intelligent transportation systems, pavement design and materials, transportation economics, traffic safety), or water resources (e.g., earthscape processes, environmental and biological systems, hydrologic and climate dynamics, hydrodynamics and turbulence). 

The MS thesis (Plan A) option combines coursework with research and development. The project-based (Plan B) option offers students an opportunity to include up to 3 credits of directed research in their coursework program. The coursework-only (Plan C) option is for practicing engineers who wish to pursue the MS part-time, self-funded, and full-time students, as well as students interested in pursuing the PhD.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": true, "cdProgramDualDegCredInCommonMax": 18, "cdProgramDualDegMemo": [{"bucketName": "coursedog-static-public", "name": "CEGE Dual Degree.docx", "path": "files/form/1703260186796/2oteihqgQ9vZ.docx"}], "cdProgramDualDegTitle": "MS-Civil Engineering/MSISyE (Transportation Engineering Focus):  15 common credits 
MS-Civil Engineering/MURP (Transportation or Environmental Engineering Focus):  18 common credits", "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Math, Engineering, and Science", "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "3.000", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Research", "cdProgramLOs": "<ul><li><p>demonstrate advanced knowledge in core areas of civil engineering</p></li><li><p>communicate effectively across disciplines and with a range of audiences</p></li><li><p>understand ethical norms and conduct research with integrity</p></li><li><p>possess the skills necessary for collaborative teamwork and leadership roles in academia, industry, or related fields</p></li></ul>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "4", "cdProgramMastPlanA": true, "cdProgramMastPlanACrMax": "20", "cdProgramMastPlanACrMin": "20", "cdProgramMastPlanACrThesis": "10", "cdProgramMastPlanAFinExDesc": "Oral examination only", "cdProgramMastPlanB": true, "cdProgramMastPlanBCap": true, "cdProgramMastPlanBCapDescr": "The Plan B requires completion of 1 to 3 Plan B papers as determined by the faculty advisor. Plan B papers can include computer programs, annotated bibliographies, field investigations, and analysis/design of special engineering problems. ", "cdProgramMastPlanBCrMax": "30", "cdProgramMastPlanBCrMin": "30", "cdProgramMastPlanBFinEx": "Oral examination only", "cdProgramMastPlanC": true, "cdProgramMastPlanCCap": true, "cdProgramMastPlanCCapDesc": "The Plan C requires 100 hours of project work and an oral presentation. ", "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMax": "30", "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMin": "30", "cdProgramMastPlanCHasFinEx": false, "cdProgramMinorAllowed": true, "cdProgramNameShort": "Civil Engr", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Master's", "cdProgramWebsite": "https://cse.umn.edu/cege", "feV9J": "<p>Required for Fall 2026 catalog update</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCED - College of Education and Human Development", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "15", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The sports coaching minor offers an in-depth study of the theoretical and applied nature of coaching through an integrated series of courses based on national standards. The educational objectives of the Minor in Sports Coaching are for students to acquire knowledge of the theoretical foundations of sports coaching and the growth and development of athletes. The psychological, social, biological, and physical aspects of risk management and athletic injury will also be addressed throughout the curriculum. 

Students will gain an understanding of training and conditioning programs for health and/or performance and practice in program design. Students will learn general training principles, such as periodization, adaptation, accommodation, specificity/overload, and explore effective training of the energy systems involved in physical activity and sport. Additionally, students will learn about the muscular and neuromuscular systems, physiological and performance adaptations to training, measurement and evaluation of fitness, and strategies for maximizing adaptation to training. Students will learn to apply this knowledge to improving health and/or performance in sport and physical fitness.

Students will also develop a philosophical approach and foundational knowledge that academically prepares them to coach at any competitive level and context including schools, sports clubs, community programs, colleges, or other athletic organizations.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramLOs": "<p><em>Students pursuing this minor will take coursework that support the development of their proficiency in:</em><br></p><p><em>-Mastering a body of knowledge and a mode of inquiry</em></p><p><em>-Locating and critically evaluating information</em></p><p><em>-Communicating effectively</em></p><p><em>-Identifying, defining and solving problems,</em></p><p><em>-Understanding diverse philosophies and cultures</em></p><p><em>-Understanding the role of creativity, innovation, discovery and expression</em></p><p><em>-Acquiring skills for effective citizenship and lifelong learning.</em></p>", "cdProgramMinorType": "Free-standing minor", "cdProgramNameShort": "SportCoach", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Undergraduate minor", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><strong>Advising</strong><br><strong>High school students</strong><br><a href="https://admissions.tc.umn.edu/apply" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Contact the University Admissions Office</a><br>&nbsp;<br><strong>Transfer students</strong><br><a href="https://www.cehd.umn.edu/undergraduate/transfer" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">CEHD Transfer Admissions</a><br><a href="mailto:cehdtrfr@umn.edu" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">cehdtrfr@umn.edu</a><br>&nbsp;<br><strong>University of Minnesota students</strong><br><a href="https://www.cehd.umn.edu/undergraduate/transfer/internal" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Application to transfer</a>&nbsp;from within the U of M system<br><a href="mailto:cehdtrfr@umn.edu" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">cehdtrfr@umn.edu</a><br>&nbsp;<br><strong>CEHD students</strong><br>Call or email CEHD Student Services to schedule an appointment with your student services advisor.<br>Phone:&nbsp;612-625-3339<br>Email:&nbsp;<a href="mailto:cehdinfo@umn.edu" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">cehdinfo@umn.edu</a><br>&nbsp;<br><strong>Sports Coaching minors</strong><br>School of Kinesiology<br>Phone: 612-625-5300 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Fax: 612-626-7700<br>Email: <a href="mailto:kin@umn.edu" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">kin@umn.edu</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>2026-2028 Catalog Update</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "reportAsNscProgram": false, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "Note:  The Comparative Literature graduate program does not accept applications directly to the MA; rather, the MA is an additional or alternative credential for students admitted to the Comparative Literature PhD program.", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLReadScore": "19", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": "79", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLWritScore": "21", "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "30", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Note: The Comparative Literature (CL) graduate program does not accept applications directly to the MA; rather, the MA is an additional or alternative credential for students admitted to the Comparative Literature PhD program. 

Comparative literature is the oldest field of literary criticism, dating back to the seventeenth century. Among the wide range of studies currently conducted in comparative literature nationally and internationally, this program focuses on theories of literary criticism and its explanatory bases; indeed, the program is seen as one of the principal initiators of such fields of study. This program is likewise engaged in pushing the bounds of critical inquiry in related domains of literary studies, directing much of its energies toward the intersection of literature with other media (in various constellations of word, sound, and image) and literatures of the global North with those of the global South, engaging problems ranging from narrative to postcolonial studies. The curriculum emphasizes seminars and directed research.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "3.500", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Research", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>The department is a preeminent site for integrating conceptual, historical, literary, and philosophical scholarship. We engage literature, culture, and thought across Africa, the Americas, Europe, and Asia. Our graduate programs in comparative literature emphasize the importance of reading tradition against the grain—of national boundaries, textual practices, and intellectual production in the humanities. We conceive literature broadly—as a field of interrelation between word, image, and sound—and comparatism as a polycentric enterprise. Over the years, graduates of our program have been very successful at carrying forward our department’s critical comparatism and signature stress on word, image, and sound in their own scholarship. Our faculty teach seminars that explore a wide range of literary and cultural problems, embedding the understanding of texts within their material and discursive conditions of possibility. We regard comparatism as the heart and soul of cross-cultural inquiry and understanding and encourage students to pursue interdisciplinary projects that are at the same time disciplined and critical.&nbsp;</p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "6", "cdProgramMastPlanA": false, "cdProgramMastPlanB": true, "cdProgramMastPlanBCap": true, "cdProgramMastPlanBCapDescr": "Passing the Comparative Literature doctoral preliminary written and oral examinations, or completing one Plan B paper of approximately 40 pages.
", "cdProgramMastPlanBCrMax": 30, "cdProgramMastPlanBCrMin": 30, "cdProgramMastPlanBFinEx": "Oral examination only", "cdProgramMastPlanC": false, "cdProgramMinorAllowed": false, "cdProgramNameShort": "Compa Lit", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": true, "cdProgramReqLanguageGradDescr": "Proficiency in two languages (other than English) ", "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Master's", "cdProgramWebsite": "https://cla.umn.edu/cscl/graduate", "feV9J": "<p>Fall 2026: Deleted CSCL 5401 and CSCL 5666 (inactive courses) from Major Electives; otherwise no changes. Rebuilt the Program Requirement section into separate blocks. Updated Learning Outcomes.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": false, "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "Students interested in the minor are strongly encouraged to confer with their major field advisor and director of graduate studies, and the Comparative Literature director of graduate studies regarding feasibility and requirements.
", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": false, "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDoctMinorMax": 12, "cdProgramCreditsDoctMinorMin": "12", "cdProgramCreditsMastMinorMax": 9, "cdProgramCreditsMastMinorMin": "9", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Comparative literature is the oldest field of literary criticism, dating back to the seventeenth century. Among the wide range of studies currently conducted in comparative literature nationally and internationally, this program focuses on theories of literary criticism and its explanatory bases; indeed, the program is seen as one of the principal initiators of such fields of study. 

This program is likewise engaged in pushing the bounds of critical inquiry in related domains of literary studies, directing much of its energies toward the intersection of literature with other media (in various constellations of word, sound, and image) and of literatures of the global North with those of the global South, engaging problems ranging from narrative to postcolonial studies. The curriculum emphasizes seminars and directed research. ", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": 3.5, "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": false, "cdProgramGradMinorLevel": "Both Masters and Doctoral", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>Students who complete the graduate minor in Comparative Literature:</p><p>- Can identify, define, and solve problems.</p><p>- Can locate and critically evaluate information.</p><p>- Have mastered a body of knowledge and a mode of inquiry.</p><p>- Understand diverse philosophies and cultures within and across societies.</p><p>- Can communicate effectively.</p><p>- Understand the role of creativity, innovation, discovery, and expression across disciplines.</p><p>- Have acquired skills for effective citizenship and lifelong learning.</p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "8", "cdProgramMinorType": "Minor related to a major", "cdProgramNameShort": "Compa Lit", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": true, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Graduate minor", "cdProgramWebsite": "https://cla.umn.edu/cscl/graduate", "feV9J": "<p>Fall 2026: Deleted inactive courses CSCL 5401 and CSCL 5666 from Major Electives.  Also, I needed to rebuild the Program Requirements section to match GPAS. Added Learning Outcomes text.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "reportAsNscProgram": false, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": true, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "Oin5M": ["All 50 US States"], "RF2TB": "Kathleen Hill Gallant", "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "YUCsS": "https://fscn.cfans.umn.edu/graduate/dietetics", "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCOA - College of Food, Agricultural and Natural Resource Sciences", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionDegreeUgrd": "Dietetics Master of Professional Studies (MPS) applicants are required to have successfully completed an ACEND-accredited undergraduate Didactic Program in Dietetics (DPD). Fall admission only.", "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": "3", "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSReadScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSWritScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "Admitted MPS applicants will be required to submit their DPD Verification Statement to the MPS Graduate Program Coordinator prior to the first day of classes of the term for which they are admitted.

In lieu of a DPD Verification Statement, admitted applicants can submit a signed approval form from the University of Minnesota-Twin Cities Department of Food Science and Nutrition DPD Program Director. More information about the DPD Program Director Approval Form can be found on the application itself and on the document found at z.umn.edu/MPSAppInstructions.", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLReadScore": "19", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": "79", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLWritScore": "21", "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "30", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The Dietetics Master of Professional Studies (MPS) focuses on coursework that combines advanced knowledge of nutrition, dietetics, and evidence-based practice. Graduates will be positioned to interpret and communicate evidence-based nutrition concepts to socioeconomically and culturally diverse audiences including content experts, educators, media, patients/clients, and the public.

The Dietetics MPS Program was developed for students intending to pursue a career in dietetics and plan to sit for the Entry-level Registration Examination for Dietitians, colloquially referred to as the RDN Exam. The expected time to degree for a Dietetics MPS student is typically one year. A student completing the Dietetics MPS program in one year will take two 15-credit semesters. The Dietetics MPS is considered the terminal degree for registered dietitians.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Math, Engineering, and Science", "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "2.800", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Professional", "cdProgramLOs": "The Dietetics MPS Program utilizes the University student learning outcomes for program quality and assessment. By completing this program, students develop a strong foundation in clinical nutrition, therapeutic dietary interventions, and food service management.", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": 2, "cdProgramMastPlanA": false, "cdProgramMastPlanB": false, "cdProgramMastPlanC": true, "cdProgramMastPlanCCap": false, "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMax": 30, "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMin": 30, "cdProgramMastPlanCHasFinEx": false, "cdProgramMinorAllowed": true, "cdProgramNameShort": "Dietetics", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Master's", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://fscn.cfans.umn.edu/graduate/dietetics" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://fscn.cfans.umn.edu/graduate/dietetics</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>Typos in public facing documentation are not preferred.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": false, "saaWhifDispPrem": false, "saaWhifDispStd": false, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active", "yNvRc": "hillkm@umn.edu"}

{"JBsh4": true, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "Oin5M": ["Minnesota"], "RF2TB": "Molly Bendzick", "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "YUCsS": "https://ci.umn.edu/masters", "a3nTH": true, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCED - College of Education and Human Development", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionDegreeUgrd": "A bachelor's degree from an accredited college or university. ", "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "Test of English as a Foreign language (TOEFL)", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": "2.8", "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "Applicants must submit transcripts from every college attended (even those where a degree wasn't earned), scores from the TOEFL/IELTS/MELAB (if applicable), a resume, and a clearly written statement of career interests, goals, and objectives. Master's applications are reviewed by department faculty three times per academic year: Fall, Spring and Summer.
", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLReadScore": "19", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": "79", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLWritScore": "21", "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "30", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom", "Hybrid"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": ["Partially Online"], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The Master of Education (MEd)/professional studies degree programs are designed to meet the needs of practicing professionals in education and human development fields. Students admitted typically have interests in improving their current professional practice and applying their education to their present work responsibilities.  

Earning a teacher certification at the UMN Twin Cities in a state-approved licensure program is part of the entry-level process for working full-time in a Minnesota school. Other states have unique criteria for certifying teachers.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "2.800", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Professional", "cdProgramLOs": "The Working Professionals M.Ed program offers students the opportunity to earn a Minnesota state teaching license.  As such, the program uses the outcomes established in administrative rule by Minnesota’s Professional Educator Licensing and Standards Board (PELSB). These are robust outcomes, organized as general pedagogy standards and language teaching knowledge and methodology standards.", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "10", "cdProgramMastPlanA": false, "cdProgramMastPlanB": false, "cdProgramMastPlanC": true, "cdProgramMastPlanCCap": false, "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMax": "30", "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMin": "30", "cdProgramMastPlanCHasFinEx": false, "cdProgramMinorAllowed": true, "cdProgramNameShort": "Curr/Instr", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": true, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": true, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Master's", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://ci.umn.edu/masters-professional-studies">https://ci.umn.edu/masters-professional-studies</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>Programs that are closing have either already stopped admissions or were not enrolling enough students.  We have other programs that will fit their needs.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active", "yNvRc": "mollyb@umn.edu"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCED - College of Education and Human Development", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": true, "cdProgramAccreditedName": "Approved by National Association of School Psychologists (NASP)", "cdProgramAccreditedNextRev": "2024-04-01", "cdProgramAccreditedRevFreq": "Every 7 years", "cdProgramAccreditedWebsite": "www.nasponline.org", "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": "3", "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "Applicants must apply online submitting a department application, resume or curriculum vita, three letters of recommendation, and a personal statement and essay following program guidelines specified on website.

Applications should be accompanied by official transcripts from all colleges and universities attended. An interview is also required for those who make the initial cut.

Applications are accepted for fall admission only (deadline December 1).", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLReadScore": "19", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": "79", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLWritScore": "21", "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMax": 60, "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "60", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Students' graduate preparation focuses on the knowledge and skills necessary to engage in the provision of research-based school psychological practices within multi-tier systems of support to improve academic, social, behavioral, and emotional competence of children and youth, as well as to develop, implement, and use applied research in school settings. Students develop specific competencies through a broad range of didactic courses, research activities, and field placements, including practica and a full-year internship. The specialist certificate (EdS) is designed for students who want to become practitioners. The school psychology specialist certificate is approved by the Minnesota Board of Teaching and the National Association of School Psychologists. Graduates are eligible for the Minnesota school psychologist credential and the national certification in school psychology, as well as the school psychology credential in most states.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "2.8", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": false, "cdProgramLOs": "<p>Student Learning Outcomes</p><ol type="number"><li><p>Research</p></li><li><p>Ethical and Legal Standards</p></li><li><p>Individual and Cultural Diversity</p></li><li><p>Professional Values and Attitutes</p></li><li><p>Communication and Interpersonal Skills</p></li><li><p>Assessment</p></li><li><p>Intervention</p></li><li><p>Consultation and Interprofessional Skills<br></p></li></ol>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "8", "cdProgramMinorAllowed": false, "cdProgramNameShort": "Educ Psy", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Post-baccalaureate credit certificate/licensure/endorsement", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://edpsych.umn.edu/academics/school-psychology" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://edpsych.umn.edu/academics/school-psychology</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>On March 13, 2025, the school psychology program voted unanimously to modify the SC and PhD program requirement to accommodate the course changes and additions, specifically increasing the credits required for EPSY 8812 from 3 credits to 6 credits and requiring EPSY 8814 for the specialist (3 credits) and PhD students (6 credits). These additions are intended to align credit-bearing learning experiences with support for competency development and other outcomes under the program’s accreditation requirements to minimize consequential learning experiences not associated with credit enrollment and associated accountability. This will in turn support for licensure and timely degree completion, which are monitored annually by APA.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TIOT - College of Science and Engineering", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": "3.4", "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "Applications for admission are considered for both fall and spring. Deadline for fall is December 1st and spring is October 1st.  Applications submitted after deadlines will be considered if space is available.

The GRE test is not required.
", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLReadScore": "19", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLSpeakScore": 23, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": "79", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLWritScore": "21", "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "64", "cdProgramCreditsDoctMajMax": 40, "cdProgramCreditsDoctMajMin": 40, "cdProgramCreditsDoctThesis": "24", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom", "Hybrid"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": ["Partially Online"], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering offers diverse educational programs that encompass nearly all aspects of modern electrical and computer engineering, ranging from the very theoretical system and information theory to highly experimental work in novel device research and microelectronics. Emphases in the major are solid state and physical electronics, surface physics, thin films, sputtering, noise and fluctuation phenomena, quantum electronics, plasma physics, automation, power systems and power electronics theory, wave propagation, communication systems and theory, optics, lasers, fiber optics, magnetism, semiconductor properties and devices, VLSI and WSI engineering in theory and practice, network theory, signal and image processing, and computer and systems engineering. Interdisciplinary work is also available in bioelectrical sciences, control sciences, computer sciences, solar energy, applications of systems theory to urban transportation and economic planning, and biological modeling.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Math, Engineering, and Science", "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "3.3", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Research", "cdProgramLOs": "<ul><li><p>Demonstrate advanced knowledge in core and emerging areas of Electrical Engineering and/or Computer Engineering</p></li><li><p>Formulate significant research questions and hypotheses to design and conduct independent, original research</p></li><li><p>Communicate effectively including across disciplines and with a range of technical and non-technical audiences</p></li><li><p>Apply ethical norms and conduct research with professional integrity</p></li></ul>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "10", "cdProgramMinorAllowed": true, "cdProgramNameShort": "Elec Engr", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Doctorate", "cdProgramWebsite": "https://cse.umn.edu/ece", "feV9J": "<p>2026-28 catalog update</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TIOT - College of Science and Engineering", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": true, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseMin": 6, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "Yes, freshmen and transfer students", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "120", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": 84, "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Environmental geoscience is the study of processes within, and interactions between, the atmosphere, the ocean, and the land that determine the habitability and sustainability of the planet. In short, it is the branch of geoscience that is concerned with the interactions between humans and the geologic environment. The subject covers natural processes that have been modifying the planet over its entire history but with a strong focus on understanding the modern system and how it has been affected by human activities. Students earning a major in environmental geoscience will develop key observational and analytical skills that enable them to address fundamental questions about the functioning of geoscience systems, especially in relation to hydrology and water quality, soils, mineral resources, and climate change. 

Students who earn an Earth Sciences B.S. may not earn an Environmental Geosciences B.S. degree.

This major is well suited to those interested in pursuing geoscience careers in environmental, geological, and hydrogeological consulting, industry, and local, state, and federal government agencies. In addition to acquiring a foundation in the geologic processes that govern water, soil, and natural resource development, students will gain a range of transferable skills, including written and oral reports, critical analysis and interpretation of data, and group work.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Math, Engineering, and Science", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>The Department of Earth and Environmental Sciences has defined the following learning outcomes as motivated by the University Student Learning Outcomes. 1) Students will  identify, define, and solve a range of fundamental problems in Earth Sciences. 2) Students will demonstrate the ability to locate and collect data relevant to problems in Earth Sciences and to analyze those data in an appropriate manner. 3) Students will demonstrate the ability to apply knowledge of Earth Sciences, including the processes of the integrated Earth system and the history of the Earth system, at an appropriate level of sophistication and rigor. 4) Students will communicate scientific information and ideas effectively to various audiences in appropriate modes (writing, verbally, visually).</p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsUndergrad": "8", "cdProgramNameShort": "Env Geosci", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Baccalaureate", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cse.umn.edu/esci" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">cse.umn.edu/esci</a></p>", "cdSamplePlanLink": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://sampleplan.umn.edu/node/691">Environmental Geosciences B S E G</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>Rationale Three considerations drive the proposed changes. Reducing financial barriers for students. The current requirements include two summer field courses, which obligate students to pay summer tuition outside their standard financial aid packages. The campus head of financial aid has expressed strong support for removing this summer course requirement given the documented difficulties it creates for aid-receiving students. The proposed ESCI 3996 uses the “extended regular session” option to deliver 10 days of presemester field instruction combined with an in-semester component, all covered by regular fall semester tuition and eligible for standard financial aid. Modernizing the capstone to reflect the breadth of the discipline. Our Advisory Board emphasized that successful careers in Earth and environmental sciences increasingly draw on a diverse skill set including data management, data analytics, and laboratory analytical approaches in addition to traditional field skills. The current model, in which a summer field course serves as the defacto capstone for BS students, does not accommodate this breadth. The proposed capstone offers a defined menu of options spanning field, laboratory, and computational research experiences, while preserving field-based options for students who choose them. Aligning BA and BS requirements. The BS programs currently require a capstone experience andmeetthatrequirementthroughESCI4911orESCI4971W.TheBSprogramsarenotformally requiredtoincludeacapstone,butweviewthecapstoneexperienceassufficientlyvaluablethat we propose to require it across all four undergraduate programs uniformly.</p>", "g9JMc": true, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Undergraduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": false, "saaWhifDispPrem": false, "saaWhifDispStd": false, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

