Applications for admission are considered for both fall and spring. Deadline for fall is January 1st and spring is October 1st.  Applications submitted after deadlines will be considered if space is available.

The GRE test is not required.", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLReadScore": "19", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": "79", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLWritScore": "21", "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "30", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "30", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom", "Hybrid"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": ["Partially Online"], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering offers diverse educational programs that encompass nearly all aspects of modern electrical and computer engineering, ranging from the very theoretical system and information theory to highly experimental work in novel device research and microelectronics. Emphases in the major are solid state and physical electronics, surface physics, thin films, sputtering, noise and fluctuation phenomena, quantum electronics, plasma physics, automation, power systems and power electronics theory, wave propagation, communication systems and theory, optics, lasers, fiber optics, magnetism, semiconductor properties and devices, VLSI and WSI engineering in theory and practice, network theory, signal and image processing, and computer and systems engineering. Interdisciplinary work is also available in bioelectrical sciences, control sciences, computer sciences, solar energy, applications of systems theory to urban transportation and economic planning, and biological modeling.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Math, Engineering, and Science", "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "3", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Research", "cdProgramLOs": "<ul><li><p>Demonstrate advanced knowledge in core and emerging areas of Electrical Engineering and/or Computer Engineering</p></li><li><p>Communicate effectively including across disciplines and with a range of technical and non-technical audiences</p></li><li><p>Apply ethical norms and conduct research with professional integrity</p></li></ul>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "6", "cdProgramMastPlanA": true, "cdProgramMastPlanACrMax": 20, "cdProgramMastPlanACrMin": 20, "cdProgramMastPlanACrThesis": "10", "cdProgramMastPlanAFinExDesc": "Oral examination only", "cdProgramMastPlanB": false, "cdProgramMastPlanC": true, "cdProgramMastPlanCCap": true, "cdProgramMastPlanCCapDesc": "The Plan C project requirement can be satisfied by taking EE 8965 (3 credits) or through completion of specific major field courses. Advisor approval is required.", "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMax": 30, "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMin": 30, "cdProgramMastPlanCHasFinEx": false, "cdProgramMinorAllowed": true, "cdProgramNameShort": "E/C Engr", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Master's", "cdProgramWebsite": "https://cse.umn.edu/ece", "feV9J": "<p>2026-28 catalog update</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": false, "saaWhifDispPrem": false, "saaWhifDispStd": false, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": "3.5", "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "7.5", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "Required admission materials include three letters of recommendation; a short essay explaining scholarly, professional, and personal goals and reason(s) for choosing the University of Minnesota; and a writing sample, such as a course paper. Candidates for all degrees are admitted fall semester only; all materials must be received by December 15th.", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": "105", "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "30", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Over the past 20 years, the field of English studies has changed dramatically from a discipline concerned with studying the literary works produced by English speakers in Britain and the United States to encompass writings in English from around the globe. The concerns of literary scholars have broadened to include not only textual analyses but also cultural, social, political, and economic contexts. The field of literature itself now encompasses not only the traditional genres of poetry, prose (fiction and belles-lettres), and drama, but also extra-literary discourses: popular culture, film, television, legal documents, conduct books, and manifestos. The Department of English has been in the forefront of interdisciplinary projects, thanks to the efforts of a faculty committed to research in American studies, medieval studies, feminist studies, film studies, and cultural studies. At the same time, the department maintains the core concerns of the discipline, the traditional study of the literatures and languages in English, as well as develops writers for the present and future through the master of fine arts in creative writing degree. The department is engaged in two simultaneous projects: to preserve the core curriculum and to re-imagine its future shape.

The department offers a master of arts in English language and literature. The MA offers training in the areas of literary history, literary theory and interpretation, language, linguistics, rhetoric, and composition. Students in the MA can develop specific concentrations through consultation with the director of graduate studies.

Course requirements for the MA program are broadly defined, allowing the student to shape a personal program of study. The English program encourages and supports interdisciplinary work. ", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "3", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Research", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>1. Students will learn to understand and deploy the fundamentals of literary scholarship, including close reading, literary historical analysis, concepts of genre, and cultural critique.<br>2. Through graduate coursework in English, students will become familiar with works of literature in a range of genres and historical periods, as well as interpretive and methodological debates within literary studies.<br>3. By participating in seminar discussions, presenting scholarly work to a community of their graduate peers, and reading scholarship extensively and intensively, students will learn to convey complex ideas clearly and effectively, both orally and in writing.<br>4. Students will learn to formulate, defend, and cogently articulate original and critically significant arguments about English literature based on thoughtful analysis of primary and secondary materials.</p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "6", "cdProgramMastPlanA": false, "cdProgramMastPlanB": true, "cdProgramMastPlanBCap": true, "cdProgramMastPlanBCapDescr": "The Plan B project is made up of three Plan B papers. Each is a tightly argued essay of about 5,000 words, usually a reworking of a paper done originally for a course.", "cdProgramMastPlanBCrMax": 30, "cdProgramMastPlanBCrMin": 30, "cdProgramMastPlanBFinEx": "Oral examination only", "cdProgramMastPlanC": false, "cdProgramMinorAllowed": true, "cdProgramNameShort": "Engl", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": true, "cdProgramReqLanguageGradDescr": "A reading knowledge of one language.", "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Master's", "cdProgramWebsite": "https://cla.umn.edu/english", "feV9J": "<p>Fall 2026: Updated the Program Learning Outcomes section. Otherwise, no changes.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TPUB - School of Public Health", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": true, "cdProgramAccreditedName": "Council on Education for Public Health", "cdProgramAccreditedNextRev": "2029-06-01", "cdProgramAccreditedRevFreq": "Every 7 years", "cdProgramAccreditedWebsite": "http://ceph.org", "cdProgramAdmissionDegreeUgrd": "Minimum requirements include a baccalaureate degree with coursework in the basic sciences. ", "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": "3", "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "7", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "For TOEFL exams taken before January 21, 2026, the minimum preferred total score of 100, and exams taken
on or after January 21, 2026, have a preferred minimum total score of 5.", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": 5, "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": 33, "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "30", "cdProgramCreditsDoctMajMax": "84", "cdProgramCreditsDoctMajMin": "12", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Environmental health is the study of how exposures to external hazards, including chemical, physical, and biological agents, affect human health. Environmental health researchers and professionals seek to understand how to evaluate exposures that create risks to human health, how those exposures elicit biological responses that lead to disease and injury, and how policy is developed and used to prevent adverse health effects. Environmental Health at the University of Minnesota offers master's and doctoral degrees, conducts research in diverse areas of environmental health, offers continuing education, and conducts outreach. Students are prepared to be leaders in environmental health in academia, industry, consulting groups, and government agencies. The program's training and research emphasize the importance of translating basic scientific knowledge into solutions for current societal problems and concerns. 

The School of Public Health is accredited by the Council on Education for Public Health (CEPH).", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "3", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Research", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>The Council on the Education of Public Health (CEPH) is the accrediting body for the School of Public Health, and has assessment requirements for accreditation including the following areas of foundational knowledge. The School is in good standing on these assessment measures and was reaccredited in April 2022.<br><br>1. Explain public health history, philosophy and values  <br>2. Identify the core functions of public health and the 10 Essential Services  <br>3. Explain the role of quantitative and qualitative methods and sciences in describing and assessing a population’s health   <br>4. List major causes and trends of morbidity and mortality in the US or other community relevant to the school or program  <br>5. Discuss the science of primary, secondary and tertiary prevention in population health, including health promotion, screening, etc.  <br>6. Explain the critical importance of evidence in advancing public health knowledge   <br>7. Explain effects of environmental factors on a population’s health  <br>8. Explain biological and genetic factors that affect a population’s health  <br>9. Explain behavioral and psychological factors that affect a population’s health  <br>10. Explain the social, political and economic determinants of health and how they contribute to population health and health inequities  <br>11. Explain how globalization affects global burdens of disease  <br>12. Explain an ecological perspective on the connections among human health, animal health and ecosystem health (e.g., One Health) <br></p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "4", "cdProgramMastPlanA": true, "cdProgramMastPlanACrMax": 23, "cdProgramMastPlanACrMin": 22, "cdProgramMastPlanACrThesis": "10", "c