At this critical moment, there are no minors more important to understanding the past, navigating the present, or imagining the future than history. History minors make sense of the world through the study of ancient times, the recent past, and everything in between. Asking interesting questions about the past and examining a range of oral, written, visual, and material sources, history minors explore and explain how peoples across time and space have lived, loved, built community, warred, reconciled, and made sense of their worlds. As much as history is a window into the past, it also helps us understand ourselves, our identities, and how we have come to inhabit the moral, ethical, social, economic, political, religious, environmental, national, ethnic, racial, gender, and sexual communities we live in today. History helps us understand how our present and possible futures grow out of a very usable and interesting past. 

History minors develop all of the skills required to thrive in today's world from an understanding of the engines of change and an ability to assess and interpret conflicting evidence and interpretations, to the robust oral and written communication skills that will allow you to shape conversations in your chosen professions and communities. Regardless of your career path, a history minor will enable you to bring fresh and critical historical perspectives to the communities you live and work in. Historical thinking strengthens communities by encouraging them to think deeply about where they have come from and where they would like to go. This is a critical moment and an incredible time to declare a history minor.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramLOs": "<p>Student Learning Outcome 1: Critical Thinking—Students can locate and critically evaluate information.</p><p>Student Learning Outcome 2: Communication—Students are able to effectively communicate through written work and oral presentation.</p><p>Student Learning Outcome 3: Engaging Diversity—Students understand diverse cultures and philosophies within and across societies.</p><p>Student Learning Outcome 4: Mastery of Knowledge—Students have mastered a body of knowledge and are able to utilize a variety of modes of historical inquiry.</p>", "cdProgramMinorType": "Minor related to a major", "cdProgramNameShort": "Hist", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Undergraduate minor", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cla.umn.edu/history" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/history</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>Fall 2026 Catalog Update</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "reportAsNscProgram": false, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TPUB - School of Public Health", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "Test of English as a Foreign language (TOEFL)", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": "3", "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "7", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "For TOEFL exams taken before January 21, 2026: Minimum total score is 100
For TOEFL exams taken on or after January 21, 2026: Minimum total score is 5", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": 5, "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": 34, "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "34", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The Health Services Research, Policy and Administration (HSRPA) MS program includes a robust, multidisciplinary core curriculum that provides the foundation for conducting health services research and health analytics. Students work closely with their advisor on an area of specialization to develop a program tailored to their interests and professional needs.

The School of Public Health is accredited by the Council on Education for Public Health (CEPH).", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": true, "cdProgramDualDegCredInCommonMax": "8", "cdProgramDualDegMemo": [{"bucketName": "coursedog-static-public", "name": "temp placeholder.pdf", "path": "files/form/1707937528941/U8HO6ve56Fo9.pdf"}], "cdProgramDualDegTitle": "JD/MS-HSRP&A Students may take a total of 8 credits in common among the academic programs. ", "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Health Services", "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "3.000", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Research", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>The Council on the Education of Public Health (CEPH) is the accrediting body for the School of Public Health, and has assessment requirements for accreditation including the following areas of foundational knowledge. The School is in good standing on these assessment measures and was reaccredited in April 2022.<br><br>1. Explain public health history, philosophy, and values  <br>2. Identify the core functions of public health and the 10 Essential Services  <br>3. Explain the role of quantitative and qualitative methods and sciences in describing and assessing a population’s health   <br>4. List major causes and trends of morbidity and mortality in the US or other community relevant to the school or program  <br>5. Discuss the science of primary, secondary, and tertiary prevention in population health, including health promotion, screening, etc.  <br>6. Explain the critical importance of evidence in advancing public health knowledge   <br>7. Explain effects of environmental factors on a population’s health  <br>8. Explain biological and genetic factors that affect a population’s health  <br>9. Explain behavioral and psychological factors that affect a population’s health  <br>10. Explain the social, political and economic determinants of health and how they contribute to population health and health inequities  <br>11. Explain how globalization affects global burdens of disease  <br>12. Explain an ecological perspective on the connections among human health, animal health, and ecosystem health (e.g., One Health) </p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "2", "cdProgramMastPlanA": false, "cdProgramMastPlanB": true, "cdProgramMastPlanBCap": true, "cdProgramMastPlanBCapDescr": "The Plan B project comprises either an industry-specific project involving student collaboration with a local organization, or independent research conducted on a relevant topic of interest. The project is selected in consultation with and guided by the advisor.", "cdProgramMastPlanBCrMax": "34", "cdProgramMastPlanBCrMin": "34", "cdProgramMastPlanBFinEx": "Oral examination only", "cdProgramMastPlanC": false, "cdProgramMinorAllowed": true, "cdProgramNameShort": "HlthSrvRPA", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Master's", "cdProgramWebsite": "www.sph.umn.edu", "feV9J": "<p>Every two years programs must submit a new proposal, this serves as the proposal for Fall 2026. This proposal reflects no changes to the current curriculum for the Health Services Research, Policy, and Administration M.S.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TPUB - School of Public Health", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": true, "cdProgramAccreditedName": "Commission on Accreditation of Healthcare Management Education (CAHME)", "cdProgramAccreditedNextRev": "2027-08-21", "cdProgramAccreditedRevFreq": "Every 7 years", "cdProgramAccreditedWebsite": "https://cahme.org/", "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": 3.1, "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "7", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "For TOEFL exams taken before January 21, 2026: Minimum total score is 100
