Please note that the Residential program is offered in-person or hybrid formats while the Executive program is offered online with residencies.", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": 5, "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "60", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "42", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom", "Hybrid"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": ["Partially Online"], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The master of healthcare administration (MHA) offers both a residential program and an executive track. The residential program, delivered in a learning cohort model, is a competency-based curriculum that emphasizes deep understanding of healthcare delivery and financing institutions, problem-solving, innovation, strategic thinking, and leadership development. The executive track is delivered in a dynamic learning cohort model through online coursework with synchronous in-person and/or online kick-off sessions each semester.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": true, "cdProgramDualDegCredInCommonMax": "12", "cdProgramDualDegMemo": [{"bucketName": "coursedog-static-public", "name": "MHA_MBA Dual Degree POS - Sheet1.pdf", "path": "files/form/1709045280507/I77s4DHydYKu.pdf"}], "cdProgramDualDegTitle": "MBA/MHA-Healthcare Administration", "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Health Services", "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "3.000", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": false, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Professional", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>The Council on the Education of Public Health (CEPH) is the accrediting body for the School of Public Health, and has assessment requirements for accreditation including the following areas of foundational knowledge. The School is in good standing on these assessment measures and was reaccredited in April 2022.<br><br>1. Explain public health history, philosophy and values  <br>2. Identify the core functions of public health and the 10 Essential Services  <br>3. Explain the role of quantitative and qualitative methods and sciences in describing and assessing a population’s health   <br>4. List major causes and trends of morbidity and mortality in the US or other community relevant to the school or program  <br>5. Discuss the science of primary, secondary and tertiary prevention in population health, including health promotion, screening, etc.  <br>6. Explain the critical importance of evidence in advancing public health knowledge   <br>7. Explain effects of environmental factors on a population’s health  <br>8. Explain biological and genetic factors that affect a population’s health  <br>9. Explain behavioral and psychological factors that affect a population’s health  <br>10. Explain the social, political and economic determinants of health and how they contribute to population health and health inequities  <br>11. Explain how globalization affects global burdens of disease  <br>12. Explain an ecological perspective on the connections among human health, animal health and ecosystem health (e.g., One Health) </p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "4", "cdProgramMastPlanA": false, "cdProgramMastPlanB": false, "cdProgramMastPlanC": true, "cdProgramMastPlanCCap": true, "cdProgramMastPlanCCapDesc": "Advanced Problem Solving in Healthcare Administration, PubH 6577, 2 credits.", "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMax": "60", "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMin": 42, "cdProgramMastPlanCHasFinEx": false, "cdProgramMinorAllowed": true, "cdProgramNameShort": "HlthCareAd", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Master's", "cdProgramWebsite": "www.sph.umn.edu", "feV9J": "<p>The MHA Executive Program is proposing strategic curriculum updates to continue providing students with a premier, practical healthcare leadership educational experience. These interrelated updates address critical gaps in behavioral leadership and global health systems analysis while shifting our legal and ethics coursework toward executive risk management and ethical stewardship.&nbsp; These proposed changes were reviewed and approved by the HPM Education Committee on April 27, 2026.</p><p>Crucially, these updates maintain the program-required total of 42 credits and align strictly with the competency models of our accrediting bodies, CAHME and CEPH. Furthermore, these changes bring our executive curriculum into closer structural and pedagogical alignment with our residential track, strengthening our standing as a unified, dual-track accredited program as we head into our self-study year for accreditation.</p><p>SPH has a plan to work with currently enrolled students to opt-in to the new requirements. </p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "120", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "120", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMax": "48", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "31", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "This broad, interdisciplinary field studies Jewish history and cultures from biblical antiquity to the present. The diverse quality of Jewish civilizations and the unifying forces of religion and language offer ample material for the study of continuity, adaptation, and change.

The undergraduate program offers courses in Hebrew Bible, Second Temple Judaism, the origins and foundational texts of rabbinic Judaism, Jewish history in the ancient, medieval, and modern worlds, Jewish literature, Jewish philosophy, the Holocaust, modern Israel, and the Jewish presence in popular culture. The program has links with the Departments of Classical & Near Eastern Studies, Sociology, History, Spanish & Portuguese Studies, French & Italian Studies, English, German, Nordic, Slavic & Dutch, Political Science, and the School of Music. The University's Center for Holocaust and Genocide Studies also offers courses related to the Nazi Holocaust and its aftermath.

Jewish studies majors acquire marketable skills in critical analysis, research, and writing that are applicable across all disciplines and in a variety of professions. In addition, majors gain cross-cultural awareness and sensitivity that is sought after in the workplace, as well as a historical awareness of the complexities that define the modern world. Jewish studies majors have access to undergraduate research opportunities and receive assistance in identifying internships and other professionalizing activities.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Communications, Languages, and Cultural Studies", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>Jewish Studies uses the University student learning outcomes:</p><ul><li><p>Students can identify, define, and solve problems.</p></li><li><p>Students can locate and critically evaluate information.</p></li><li><p>Students have mastered a body of knowledge and a mode of inquiry.</p></li><li><p>Students understand diverse philosophies and culture within and across societies.</p></li><li><p>Students can communicate effectively.</p></li><li><p>Students understand the role of creativity, innovation, discovery, and expression across disciplines.</p></li><li><p>Students have acquired skills for effective citizenship and lifelong learning.</p></li></ul>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsUndergrad": "8", "cdProgramNameShort": "JewishStdy", "cdProgramReqLanguageSemesters": "4", "cdProgramReqLanguageSpecific": "Hebrew", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "Specific second languages", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Baccalaureate", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cla.umn.edu/jewish-studies" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/jewish-studies</a></p>", "cdSamplePlanLink": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://sampleplan.umn.edu/node/501">Jewish Studies B.A.</a></p>", 