Natural languages all involve a system of meaning (semantics), a system of externalization through speech or sign (phonology), and a grammatical system of sentence structure that links meaning and externalization (syntax). Students who major in linguistics gain a strong background in the principles governing the structure of natural languages in all of these areas. Other coursework addresses the role of language in human cognition and social interaction, how languages change over time, computer understanding of natural languages, methods in language documentation and analysis, and other topics.

Coursework and research activities in the Institute of Linguistics give our majors and minors both breadth and depth in linguistics, as well as opportunities to participate in cutting-edge linguistic research. Our graduates take their in-depth knowledge of how language works and their strong problem-solving skills to a variety of careers, including language policy and planning, language teaching, natural language processing and data science, publishing, translation and interpretation, advertising and branding. In addition, coursework in linguistics provides excellent preparation for graduate study in linguistics, NLP and computer science, and professional programs such as law and speech-language-hearing sciences.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramLOs": "<p>The program utilizes the following University defined student learning outcomes:&nbsp;</p><ul><li><p>Students can identify, define, and solve problems.</p></li><li><p>Students have mastered a body of knowledge and a mode of inquiry.</p></li><li><p>Students can communicate effectively.</p></li></ul>", "cdProgramMinorType": "Minor related to a major", "cdProgramNameShort": "Ling", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Undergraduate minor", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cla.umn.edu/linguistics" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/linguistics</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>fall 2026 catalog update</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "reportAsNscProgram": false, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TIOT - College of Science and Engineering", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionDegreeUgrd": "A bachelor's degree in materials science or other related field.", "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "Applicants must submit three letters of recommendation from persons familiar with their scholarship and research potential; a complete set of official transcripts; and a clearly written statement summarizing research/work experience and motivation for graduate work. 

Applications are accepted for fall semester only. December 15 is the application deadline; late applications are considered if space is available. 
", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLReadScore": "19", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": "79", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLWritScore": "21", "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "30", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Research activities in the Chemical Engineering and Materials Science (CEMS) Department focus on the development of renewable energy technologies, the solution of important medical and biological engineering challenges, the development of advanced materials and characterization methods, and the application of sophisticated mathematical and theoretical models. Graduate courses offered cover core areas of chemical engineering (fluid mechanics, applied mathematics: linear and nonlinear analysis, transport, chemical thermodynamics, statistical thermodynamics and kinetics, and analysis of chemical reactors) and core areas of materials science (structure and symmetry of materials, thermodynamics and kinetics, transport, advanced mathematics, electronic properties of materials, and mechanical properties of materials). In addition, several specialized topics are offered, including biochemical engineering, biological transport processes, colloids, principles of mass transfer in engineering and biological engineering, rheology, process control, ceramics, polymers, scattering, and electrochemical engineering.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Math, Engineering, and Science", "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "2.800", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Research", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>Students will:&nbsp;</p><ul><li><p>demonstrate advanced knowledge in core and specialized areas of chemical and materials engineering</p></li><li><p>conduct original, high-impact research</p></li><li><p>communicate about research area across disciplines</p></li><li><p>communicate and practice research with professionalism and integrity</p></li><li><p>develop the skills necessary for leadership roles in academia, industry, or related fields</p></li></ul>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "4", "cdProgramMastPlanA": true, "cdProgramMastPlanACrMax": 20, "cdProgramMastPlanACrMin": 20, "cdProgramMastPlanACrThesis": "10", "cdProgramMastPlanAFinExDesc": "Written and oral examinations", "cdProgramMastPlanB": true, "cdProgramMastPlanBCap": false, "cdProgramMastPlanBCrMax": 30, "cdProgramMastPlanBCrMin": 30, "cdProgramMastPlanBFinEx": "Written and oral examinations", "cdProgramMastPlanC": true, "cdProgramMastPlanCCap": false, "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMax": 30, "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMin": 30, "cdProgramMastPlanCHasFinEx": false, "cdProgramMinorAllowed": true, "cdProgramNameShort": "MatSci/Eng", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Master's", "cdProgramWebsite": "https://cse.umn.edu/cems", "feV9J": "<p>2026-28 catalog update.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TIOT - College of Science and Engineering", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAccreditedName": "ABET", "cdProgramAccreditedNextRev": "2025-11-01", "cdProgramAccreditedRevFreq": "Every 6 years", "cdProgramAccreditedWebsite": "http://www.abet.org", "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": true, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseMin": "9", "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "Yes, freshmen and transfer students", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "124", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMax": 110, "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "108", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDeliverySite": "Students have the option to participate in a co-operative working training which is supervised at a corporate host site.", "cdProgramDescr": "Mechanical engineering is involved in most technological activities of society and dominates many fields, including automotive, transportation, materials handling, environmental and pollution control systems, refrigeration and cryogenics, power systems design, automation, system dynamics and control, computer-aided design and manufacturing, capital equipment design, and consumer products production. A mechanical engineer may be engaged in design, development, research, testing, manufacturing, administration, marketing, consulting, or education.

When you study mechanical engineering, a strong background in mathematics, physics, and chemistry is balanced with courses in engineering science and applied engineering. The department offers several elective and cross-disciplinary programs that provide further in-depth study in designated areas of the profession. These include: system design and control; manufacturing engineering; computer-aided design; thermodynamics and heat transfer; environmental engineering; power and propulsion; materials engineering; bioengineering; and mechanical-electrical engineering.
", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Math, Engineering, and Science", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>Students can identify, formulate, and solve complex engineering problems by applying principles of engineering, science, and mathematics.<br>Students can apply engineering design to produce solutions that meet specified needs with consideration of public health, safety, and welfare, as well as global, cultural, social, environmental, and economic factors.<br>Students can communicate effectively with a range of audiences.<br>Students can recognize ethical and professional responsibilities in engineering situations and make informed judgments, which must consider the impact of engineering solutions in global, economic, environmental, and societal contexts.<br>Students can function effectively on a team whose members together provide leadership, create a collaborative and inclusive environment, establish goals, plan tasks, and meet objectives.<br>Students can develop and conduct appropriate experimentation, analyze and interpret data, and use engineering judgment to draw conclusions.<br>Students can acquire and apply new knowledge as needed, using appropriate learning strategies.</p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsUndergrad": "8", "cdProgramNameShort": "Mech Engr", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Baccalaureate", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cse.umn.edu/me" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cse.umn.edu/me</a></p>", "cdSamplePlanLink": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://sampleplan.umn.edu/node/716">Mechanical Engineering B.M.E.</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>2 year update to program</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Undergraduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TIOT - College of Science and Engineering", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionDegreeUgrd": "A four-year BS degree in engineering, science, or mathematics.", "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": "3", "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "The department offers two options for applying to the master's degree program. The standard application requires a full set of application materials and allows admission to the Plan A or Plan C degree options. The streamlined application offers an abbreviated application process and admission is only for the coursework-only master's degree (Plan C). 

Students admitted to the Plan C option are not eligible for financial support from the department. 

Applications are accepted for fall semester only. The standard application deadline is December 15 and the streamlined application deadline is April 15.", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLReadScore": 4.5, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": 4.5, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLWritScore": 4.5, "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "30", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Coursework and research are offered in advanced and additive manufacturing; bioengineering; combustion; computer-aided design; computer-aided manufacturing; control systems; energy conservation; environmental control; environmental engineering; fluid mechanics; fluid power; heat and mass transfer; machine design; manufacturing engineering; nanoengineering and nanotechnology; particle technology; plasma chemistry; plasma heat transfer; power, propulsion, and applied thermodynamics; solar energy; sprays and multiphase flows; systems dynamics; thermal energy storage; thermal environmental engineering; thermodynamics; transportation; vibration; wind energy; and interdisciplinary finite element methodology. Additional instructional and research programs can be formulated.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Math, Engineering, and Science", "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "2.800", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Research", "cdProgramLOs": "<ul><li><p>demonstrate advanced knowledge in core areas of mechanical engineering</p></li><li><p>communicate effectively across disciplines and with a range of audiences</p></li><li><p>understand ethical norms and conduct research with integrity</p></li><li><p>possess the skills necessary for collaborative teamwork and leadership roles in academia, industry, or related fields</p></li></ul>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "4", "cdProgramMastPlanA": true, "cdProgramMastPlanACrMax": "20", "cdProgramMastPlanACrMin": "20", "cdProgramMastPlanACrThesis": "10", "cdProgramMastPlanAFinExDesc": "Oral examination only", "cdProgramMastPlanB": false, "cdProgramMastPlanC": true, "cdProgramMastPlanCCap": false, "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMax": "30", "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMin": "30", "cdProgramMastPlanCHasFinEx": false, "cdProgramMinorAllowed": true, "cdProgramNameShort": "Mech Engr", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Master's", "cdProgramWebsite": "https://cse.umn.edu/me", "feV9J": "<p>Update MSME to match course list on ME PhD</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TPHR - College of Pharmacy", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": "3", "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": false, "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "Note: Students are not admitted directly to the M.S. program. See the Medicinal Chemistry Ph.D.", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": false, "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "30", "cdProgramCreditsDoctMajMax": "24", "cdProgramCreditsDoctMajMin": "24", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Note: Students are not admitted directly to the MS program. See the Medicinal Chemistry PhD or contact the director of graduate studies for more information.

The medicinal chemistry program emphasizes the application of chemical principles to research on the action of drugs on biological systems. Courses offered by the program focus on general principles of medicinal chemistry, drug design and synthesis, chemical aspects of drug metabolism, chemical mechanisms of drug toxicity and carcinogenicity, computer-assisted drug design and receptor modeling.

Students must complete a core curriculum of advanced courses in organic and medicinal chemistry, as well as credits in a minor or related field. ", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Math, Engineering, and Science", "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "2.800", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Research", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>Medicinal chemistry is a discipline that integrates concepts from several areas including chemistry, biology, and medical sciences. Therefore, the Medicinal Chemistry graduate program provides multidisciplinary training that prepares students for careers in drug development and related fields. </p><p>After completion of the M.S. degree in Medicinal Chemistry, students will have developed and demonstrated a core knowledge of the relationships between chemical structures and biological functions for the purpose of developing new drugs and advancing methods and techniques for drug discovery and development. Students will have also developed practical skills and critical problem solving abilities in drug discovery and development through pursuing one or more capstone projects. </p><p>Biennial professional development retreats further equip students with practical “soft skill” knowledge required for successful careers in the medicinal chemistry and related fields. </p><p>Students in the program are encouraged to be active citizens in the broader scientific community, engaging with members across various departments, institutions and countries. By collaborating and exchanging knowledge with a global network of academic and professional peers, students develop crucial intercultural competence. This exposure leads to comprehending varied scientific methodologies and applying those perspectives towards solving complex scientific challenges. </p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": 9, "cdProgramMastPlanA": true, "cdProgramMastPlanACrMax": "20", "cdProgramMastPlanACrMin": "20", "cdProgramMastPlanACrThesis": "10", "cdProgramMastPlanAFinExDesc": "Oral examination only", "cdProgramMastPlanB": true, "cdProgramMastPlanBCap": false, "cdProgramMastPlanBCrMax": "30", "cdProgramMastPlanBCrMin": "30", "cdProgramMastPlanBFinEx": "Oral examination only", "cdProgramMastPlanC": false, "cdProgramMinorAllowed": true, "cdProgramNameShort": "Med Chem", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Master's", "cdProgramWebsite": "z.umn.edu/medchemgrad", "feV9J": "<p>Update to student learning outcomes.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "15", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The Medieval Studies minor covers the period of the Middle Ages, between 300 and 1500 C.E., globally. It provides students with the opportunity to engage in the history, art, theater, music, literature, and languages of the period, as well as earlier cultural influences on it, through coursework in a variety of disciplines.

The program allows students to gain a more complex understanding of the Global Middle Ages through opportunities to expand their geographic focus and diversify their approaches and fields of knowledge.

