{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TPUB - School of Public Health", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": true, "cdProgramAccreditedName": "Council on Education for Public Health (CEPH).", "cdProgramAccreditedNextRev": "2029-07-01", "cdProgramAccreditedRevFreq": "Every 7 years", "cdProgramAccreditedWebsite": "ceph.org", "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": "3", "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "7", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "Applicants to the advanced-standing option must hold either an advanced degree (e.g., MS, MD, MSW) or have 3-5 years of experience directly related to maternal and child health.

For TOEFL exams taken before January 21, 2026, the minimum total score is 100,  and for exams taken on or after January 21, 2026, the minimum total score is 5.", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": 5, "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "48", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "42", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom", "Hybrid", "Completely Online"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": ["Partially Online", "Primarily Online"], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The maternal and child health MPH program focuses on public health skills development and maternal and child health content. There are two options: the standard program is for students who have limited professional experience, and the advanced standing program is for students who either have an advanced degree (e.g., MD, MS, MSW, MED) or at least three years of professional experience related to maternal and child health or public health. Students must complete a minimum of 48 credits for the standard track and a minimum of 42 credits for the advanced standing track.   

The School of Public Health is accredited by the Council on Education for Public Health (CEPH).", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "3.000", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Professional", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>The Council on the Education of Public Health (CEPH) is the accrediting body for the School of Public Health, and has assessment requirements for accreditation including the following areas of foundational knowledge. The School is in good standing on these assessment measures and was reaccredited in April 2022.<br><br>1. Explain public health history, philosophy and values  <br>2. Identify the core functions of public health and the 10 Essential Services  <br>3. Explain the role of quantitative and qualitative methods and sciences in describing and assessing a population’s health   <br>4. List major causes and trends of morbidity and mortality in the US or other community relevant to the school or program  <br>5. Discuss the science of primary, secondary and tertiary prevention in population health, including health promotion, screening, etc.  <br>6. Explain the critical importance of evidence in advancing public health knowledge   <br>7. Explain effects of environmental factors on a population’s health  <br>8. Explain biological and genetic factors that affect a population’s health  <br>9. Explain behavioral and psychological factors that affect a population’s health  <br>10. Explain the social, political and economic determinants of health and how they contribute to population health and health inequities  <br>11. Explain how globalization affects global burdens of disease  <br>12. Explain an ecological perspective on the connections among human health, animal health and ecosystem health (e.g., One Health) </p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "4", "cdProgramMastPlanA": false, "cdProgramMastPlanB": false, "cdProgramMastPlanC": true, "cdProgramMastPlanCCap": true, "cdProgramMastPlanCCapDesc": "Students complete an Integrated Learning Experience (ILE) in consultation with the advisor.", "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMax": "48", "cdProgramMastPlanCCrMin": "42", "cdProgramMastPlanCHasFinEx": false, "cdProgramMinorAllowed": true, "cdProgramNameShort": "Mtr/C Hlth", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": true, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Master's", "cdProgramWebsite": "www.sph.umn.edu", "feV9J": "<p>In keeping with the review for the Fall 2026 catalog, a new proposal was made and the curriculum reviewed. The only changes were cleaning up the electives semantically but putting in PUBH level courses, and removing an option from the methods section.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "120", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "120", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMax": 48, "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": 34, "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Students in religious studies are trained in the critical study of religious thought, practice, institutions, and communities throughout the world and across time periods. The subject of religion is by its very nature interdisciplinary, attracting interest from many perspectives, including textual and literary studies, history, sociology, anthropology, the arts, and philosophy. 

Students in the religious studies program select one of two tracks. The "religion, culture, and society" track is designed for students who seek to study religious traditions broadly or comparatively. The "texts and traditions" track is for students who seek to study a single tradition deeply, reading its foundational texts in their original language. Both tracks examine religion as a social and cultural force affecting fundamental issues of our world. All majors take courses in at least two religious traditions and develop an interdisciplinary concentration area consisting of four courses, selected from a variety of departments and focused on a theme, tradition, time period, location, practice, or set of questions. 

For more information, visit the Religious Studies website at https://cla.umn.edu/religious-studies.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Communications, Languages, and Cultural Studies", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>The Religious Studies Program has adopted the following SLOs:</p><p>Students will demonstrate skill in finding information about specific religious domains (discourses, practices, communities, and institutions) and evaluate that information using specific analytical frameworks</p><p>University SLO: Can locate and critically evaluate information</p><p>Students will demonstrate familiarity with one or more methods of analyzing religious domains (discourses, practices, communities or institutions) and the ability to apply those methods to a specific body of information related to one of these domains.</p><p>University SLO: Have mastered a body of knowledge and a mode of inquiry</p><p>Students will demonstrate acquisition of knowledge related to one or more religious traditions.</p><p>University SLO: Understand diverse philosophies and cultures within and across societies</p><p>Students will demonstrate ability to effectively communicate information about religious domains and methods of analyzing them in both written and oral forms.</p><p>University SLO: Can communicate effectively</p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsUndergrad": "8", "cdProgramNameShort": "Relig Stdy", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": true, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Baccalaureate", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cla.umn.edu/religious-studies" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/religious-studies</a></p>", "cdSamplePlanLink": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://sampleplan.umn.edu/node/561">Religious Studies B.A.</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>fall 2026 catalog update</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Undergraduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "120", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "120", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMax": "52", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "32", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Russian is the native language of some 150 million citizens of the Russian Federal Republic. It is one of the five official languages of the UN and ranks with English, Chinese, Hindi, Urdu, and Spanish as a major world language. Russian remains the unofficial lingua franca of the former Soviet republics, an indispensable communications tool across all of the Caucasus and Central Asia. Russian is a major language for scientific publication, and it is an increasingly important language for business and trade as Russian institutions, both public and private, integrate with their European and American counterparts. 

Besides a thorough grounding in the Russian language, students in the Russian major become conversant with the enormous wealth of Russian cultural heritage in literature, visual art, theater, and music. In particular, Russia has produced one of the world's most vibrant and exciting literary traditions, including the works of poets like Pushkin, Lermontov, Blok, and Akhmatova, and writers like Gogol, Turgenev, Dostoevsky, Tolstoy, and Chekhov. Despite the upheavals caused by the fall of communism, Russian literary culture remains vibrant today, and only a fraction of this fascinating contemporary work is available in translation. 

Further information on the value of a Russian major can be found at http://modules.russnet.org/why/.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Communications, Languages, and Cultural Studies", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>We use the University SLOs. Our programs primarily focus on the following of the university-wide SLOs:&nbsp;</p><ul><li><p>Critical Thinking: Can locate and critically evaluate information.</p></li><li><p>Engaging Diversity: Understand diverse philosophies and culture within and across societies.</p></li><li><p>Communication: Can communicate effectively.</p></li><li><p>Innovation &amp; Creativity: Understand the role of creativity, innovation, discovery, and expression across disciplines.</p></li></ul>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsUndergrad": "8", "cdProgramNameShort": "Russ", "cdProgramReqLanguageSemesters": "4", "cdProgramReqLanguageSpecific": "Russian", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "Specific second languages", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Baccalaureate", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cla.umn.edu/gnsd" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/gnsd</a></p>", "cdSamplePlanLink": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://sampleplan.umn.edu/node/566">Russian B.A.</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>fall 2026</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Undergraduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "120", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "120", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMax": "72", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "32", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The program develops analytical skills and methodologies needed to explore Hispanic, Hispanic-American, and Luso-Brazilian languages and cultures. The department offers two majors (Spanish studies and combined Spanish-Portuguese studies) and two minors (Spanish studies and Portuguese studies). 

It is important to note that department majors and minors are not simply Spanish and Portuguese language programs; rather, they are liberal arts programs concentrating on Spanish, Latin American, and/or Luso-Brazilian literary, cultural, and linguistics studies with language skills as the foundation. All major and minor options in this department begin with prerequisite language courses, followed by advanced language skills courses (special arrangements may be made for native speakers of Spanish or Portuguese). These are followed by critical analysis skills courses in Hispanic/Lusophone literature, culture, and linguistics that prepare students to take advanced coursework in specific areas. The major options culminate in the completion of a capstone through a SPPT 5xxx course, a PORT 5xxx course, or SPAN 3972W. 

The department strongly encourages majors and minors to study abroad in a Spanish or Portuguese speaking country or territory. Students who wish to complete department program requirements through study abroad must meet with the department advisor prior to departure. Detailed information regarding undergraduate Spanish and Portuguese studies academic issues is printed in the Undergraduate Advising Handbook (also available at https://cla.umn.edu/spanish-portuguese).", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Communications, Languages, and Cultural Studies", "cdProgramLOs": "<ol type="1"><li><p>Students can communicate effectively</p></li><li><p>Students have mastered a body of knowledge and mode of inquiry</p></li><li><p>Students can locate and critically evaluate information</p></li></ol>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsUndergrad": "8", "cdProgramNameShort": "Span/PortS", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Baccalaureate", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cla.umn.edu/spanish-portuguese" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/spanish-portuguese</a></p>", "cdSamplePlanLink": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://sampleplan.umn.edu/node/581">Spanish and Portuguese Studies B.A.</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>fall 2026 catalog update</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Undergraduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": true, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "Oin5M": ["Minnesota"], "RF2TB": "Laura Paczkowski", "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "YUCsS": "https://edpsych.umn.edu/academics/special-ed/bachelors-teach", "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCED - College of Education and Human Development", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": true, "cdProgramAccreditedName": "Professional Educator Licensing and Standards Board (PELSB)", "cdProgramAccreditedNextRev": "2031-09-01", "cdProgramAccreditedRevFreq": "Every 6 years", "cdProgramAccreditedWebsite": "https://mn.gov/pelsb/", "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": true, "cdProgramAdmissionCreditMin": "30", "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": true, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmitMin": "2.75", "cdProgramAdmissionGPARationale": "Before applying, all students must meet the minimum admission requirements.  However, meeting minimum prerequisite course, credit levels, and GPA requirements does not guarantee admission to the Special Education major.  A variety of selection criteria are used to determine admission, including but not limited to, overall GPA, number of credits completed, and success in key courses. 

The skill set required of a special education teacher is such that a higher GPA is recommended in order to increase the likelihood of successful completion of the licensure requirements.", "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": true, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": true, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUTMin": "2.75", "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransMin": "2.75", "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "120", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "120", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMax": "66", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": 56, "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDeliverySite": "Practicum experiences will be conducted at sites serving individuals with disabilities.", "cdProgramDescr": "The Bachelor of Science Degree Program in special education (BS/SE) prepares students to serve individuals with disabilities, ages birth through 21. The program emphasizes the fundamentals of special education, effective intervention strategies, and the problem-solving approach to instruction. The BS/SE undergraduate program maintains the integrity of a research-based degree program recognized nationally. The program is specifically designed for developing scientist-practitioners by focusing on the latest developments in educational research and supporting the need to make informed, data-based instructional decisions to ensure that individuals with disabilities reach their full potential. Students in this program select from one of three sub-plans, depending on their area of interest.

Earning a teacher certification at the UMN Twin Cities in a state-approved licensure program is part of the entry-level process for working full-time in a Minnesota school. Other states have unique criteria for certifying teachers.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Education", "cdProgramLOs": "Thematically, the (BS/SpEd) proposal is guided by the Liberal education Student Learner Outcomes (SLO), Student Development Outcomes (SDO), and the CEHD Teacher Education Redesign Initiative (TERI). In order to complete the (BS/SpEd), a candidate will complete the requirements of the Liberal Education degree program including the student learning outcomes that will be assessed in the major degree coursework.", "cdProgramLengthInTermsUndergrad": "8", "cdProgramNameShort": "Spec Educ", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": true, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Baccalaureate", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://edpsych.umn.edu/academics/undergraduate-programs">https://edpsych.umn.edu/academics/undergraduate-programs</a></p>", "cdSamplePlanLink": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://sampleplan.umn.edu/node/211">Special Education B.S.</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>Catalog update</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Undergraduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active", "yNvRc": "laurap@umn.edu"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "120", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "120", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMax": "37", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": 37, "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Language plays a central role in human communication, serving as a conduit for expressing personal, regional, social, and cultural identities. The Bachelor of Arts (BA) program in Speech-Language-Hearing Sciences (SLHS) stands as an internationally recognized, comprehensive, science-based degree program that offers students a broad liberal arts education in an interdisciplinary field of study. 

The curriculum of SLHS encompasses the physical, biological, and socio-behavioral aspects that influence the production and comprehension of speech and language. While these processes are often taken for granted, disruptions caused by disease, birth-related issues, or aging can significantly impact an individual's life. The field of speech-language-hearing sciences defines the parameters of normal and disordered communication, laying the research foundation for diagnosing and treating communication disorders. The SLHS program uniquely merges science, language, and communication, offering students exceptional academic preparation. It caters to those interested in physical and biological sciences, providing insights into linguistic, psychological, and neurophysiological theories and methods for diagnosing and treating voice, speech, language, and hearing disorders throughout a person's lifespan. This foundational knowledge opens pathways to diverse career opportunities, including roles such as speech-language pathologist, audiologist, psychologist, therapist, counselor, vocal coach, researcher, instructor, manager, or director in related fields. 

The program requirements for the SLHS major fulfill a number of the University’s Liberal Education (LE) requirements. Major students also have multiple options to meet the LE requirement by taking courses in related fields such as psychology, education, humanities, and social sciences. The SLHS major encourages exploration of the discipline through classes, research laboratories, and clinical observations. The major project course provides students with a service-learning opportunity, allowing hands-on experience and the application of learned concepts in a real-world setting. Collaborating with over 300 non-profit organizations in the community, students actively contribute to meaningful projects.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Social Sciences and Human Services", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>The Department of SLHS uses the following learning outcomes:</p><ul><li><p>Students can understand diverse philosophies and culture within and across societies.</p></li><li><p>Students can locate and critically evaluate information.</p></li><li><p>Students can identify, define, and solve problems.</p></li><li><p>Students can communicate effectively.</p></li><li><p>Students will have acquired skills for effective citizenship and lifelong learning.</p></li><li><p>Students will have mastered a body of knowledge and a mode of inquiry.</p></li></ul><p>In addition to the college and university SLO, the program meets the prerequisite course content areas related to SLP certification standards set forth by the Council for Clinical Certification in Audiology and Speech-Language Pathology (CFCC) and the Council on Academic Accreditation (CAA), including academic coursework requirements in Biological Sciences, Physical Sciences, Chemistry, Social Sciences and Behavioral Sciences.</p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsUndergrad": "8", "cdProgramNameShort": "Spch/Hear", "cdProgramReqLanguageSemesters": "4", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "Any second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Baccalaureate", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cla.umn.edu/slhs" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/slhs</a></p>", "cdSamplePlanLink": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://sampleplan.umn.edu/node/586">Speech-Language-Hearing Sciences B.A.</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>Fall 2026 Catalog Update</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Undergraduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionDegreeUgrd": " 
", "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": false, "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "Students interested in the minor are strongly encouraged to confer with their major field advisor and director of graduate studies, and the Studies of Science and Technology director of graduate studies regarding feasibility and requirements.", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": false, "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDoctMinorMax": 12, "cdProgramCreditsDoctMinorMin": "12", "cdProgramCreditsMastMinorMax": 7.5, "cdProgramCreditsMastMinorMin": 7.5, "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Studies of science and technology (SST) deals with a rapidly expanding field that seeks to understand the conceptual foundations, historical development, and social dimensions and context of science and technology. SST faculty are drawn from a number of research and teaching units dedicated in whole or in part to the history, philosophy, and social studies of science and technology. 

The SST graduate minor is for students from any major who want to gain a deeper understanding of the nature and development of science and technology. It can be particularly valuable for students who are planning teaching careers in science or engineering or those majoring in philosophy or history of science and technology. ", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": 3, "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": false, "cdProgramGradMinorLevel": "Both Masters and Doctoral", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>Students develop individual programs of study in consultation with the faculty and the director of graduate studies. Program learning outcomes include analytical and critical thinking about science and/or technology from more than one disciplinary perspective, with a special emphasis on innovation and creativity in scholarship, reflected in oral and written communication produced in the required graduate coursework with different faculty. Students planning teaching careers in science or engineering are a primary constituency.</p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": 4, "cdProgramMinorType": "Free-standing minor", "cdProgramNameShort": "StdySciTec", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": true, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Graduate minor", "cdProgramWebsite": "https://cla.umn.edu/mcps/research/sst-graduate-minor", "feV9J": "<p>Fall 2026: Rebuilt Program Requirements section into separate blocks and updated Learning Outcomes.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "reportAsNscProgram": false, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "For an online application or for more information about graduate education admissions, see the General Information section of this website.", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLReadScore": "19", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": "79", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLWritScore": "21", "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMax": 15, "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "15", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["Completely Online"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "Completely Online", "cdProgramDescr": "The Department of Writing Studies trains students to understand how people use written communication (digital, visual, textual) to shape the world around them, with a particular emphasis on communication in scientific and technical areas. The certificate in technical communication focuses on applying basic theory and research-driven approaches to create and adapt content to solve complex problems in technical communication workplaces. Students connect with workplace professionals through client projects, virtual and global teamwork, mentorships, and emerging technologies. These experiences enable students to develop unique strengths in digital, usability, and science/health/medical communication. 

Certificate courses are taught by graduate faculty who themselves have active research agendas in these areas. Students also have the opportunity to work with the Technical Communication Advisory Board (TCAB), a group of business leaders who provide pathways to experiential learning opportunities including networking, mentoring, and internships.

This fully online program equips professionals for transition to the technical communication field and/or serves as the foundation for specialized study at the master's level that is tailored to career goals. All coursework from the Certificate can be applied to the MS in Technical Communication upon admission to the MS and with program approval.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "2.8", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": false, "cdProgramLOs": "<p>In the Graduate Certificate in Technical Communication program at the University of Minnesota, students apply research-driven approaches to create and adapt content to solve complex problems in technical communication workplaces.<br><br>Students connect with workplace professionals through client projects, virtual and global teamwork, mentorships, and emerging technologies. These experiences enable students to develop unique strengths in digital, usability, and science/health/medical communication.<br><br>As part of these experience-driven programs, students do the following:<br>-Understand the history, professionalism, and discourse of the field, including the ability to apply rhetorical theory/principles to scientific and technical communication;<br>-Visualize, write, design, and manage content for multiple media and contexts;<br>-Lead local/virtual/global teams in the design, development, and evaluation of scientific and technical communication;<br>-Plan and conduct research to meet user and strategic work needs;<br>-Construct and foster connected, collaborative, interactive environments; and<br>-Apply technical communication research to a specific industry (e.g., health, technology, usability, public policy, education).<br><br>These learning outcomes were collaboratively developed by program leadership and instructors.</p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": 3, "cdProgramMinorAllowed": false, "cdProgramNameShort": "Tech Comm", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqOther": "Students must obtain grades of C- or better in all courses for the degree.", "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": true, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Post-baccalaureate credit certificate/licensure/endorsement", "cdProgramWebsite": "https://cla.umn.edu/writing-studies", "feV9J": "<p>Fall 2026: Change the Certificate minimum grade requirement to match the Graduate School’s requirements to C- or better. Adding Learning Outcomes and assessment section.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "16", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The Department of Writing Studies offers a Minor in Technical Writing and Communication (TWC).

The TWC Minor enhances your degree program with professional writing and communication skills. These skills can be transferred to careers in a wide range of fields, including science and technology industries.

The minor provides theoretical and practical information about how to communicate complex technical information to various audiences. Courses in this minor especially focus on building skills in professional writing, editing, and presentation/visual display design. ", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Communications, Languages, and Cultural Studies", "cdProgramLOs": "<ul><li><p>Students will use theories, concepts, and methods from writing, rhetoric, and technical communication to identify, define, and solve problems.</p></li><li><p>Students will use research methods to gather, evaluate, and distill information from texts, subject matter experts, users, and other relevant sources.&nbsp;</p></li><li><p>Students will communicate for, about, and with diverse communities with empathy, humility, and understanding.</p></li><li><p>Students will adapt content for new genres, modalities, audiences, and contexts by strategically aligning with and deviating from conventions.</p></li><li><p>Students will recognize how writing, rhetoric, and technologies powerfully shape the world, for better and worse.&nbsp;</p></li><li><p>Students will use writing as a tool for innovation, creativity, discovery, and expression.</p></li></ul>", "cdProgramMinorType": "Minor related to a major", "cdProgramNameShort": "TecWritCom", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Undergraduate minor", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cla.umn.edu/writing-studies" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/writing-studies</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>fall 2026 catalog update</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "reportAsNscProgram": false, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": true, "cdProgramAccreditedName": "National Association of Schools of Theatre (NAST).", "cdProgramAccreditedNextRev": "2026-10-15", "cdProgramAccreditedRevFreq": "Every year", "cdProgramAccreditedWebsite": "https://nast.arts-accredit.org/", "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "Applicants must demonstrate working knowledge and reading proficiency of at least one foreign language or sign language.

", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLReadScore": "19", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": "79", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLWritScore": "21", "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "54", "cdProgramCreditsDoctMajMax": 30, "cdProgramCreditsDoctMajMin": 30, "cdProgramCreditsDoctThesis": "24", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The University of Minnesota offers a unique graduate program drawing from the varied research expertise of its core faculty. Together, the faculty is committed to the study of theatre and performance as practices of social, cultural, and political consequence. The department's work in theatre historiography and performance criticism examines the stakes of acts of representation, movement, and meaning-production both within and without the discipline of theatre. The curriculum of this program trains students to be rigorous scholars and expert teachers of theatre and performance studies at the college level. ", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "3.5", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": false, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Research", "cdProgramLOs": "As a PhD program where each student undertakes substantial independent research, we define “student learning” very differently than the university does at the undergraduate level.

Our PhD handbook states: “The Graduate Programs seek to produce scholars and artists of the highest caliber, preparing them for careers in professional and/or academic theatre and related artistic fields.” The specific outcomes of this preparation include the ability to conduct original research in theatre and/or performance studies, master bodies of information and conceptual approaches in these scholarly fields, develop a course syllabus, deliver scholarly lectures, and teach undergraduate students.", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "6", "cdProgramMinorAllowed": true, "cdProgramNameShort": "Th Arts", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": true, "cdProgramReqLanguageGradDescr": "Proficiency in one foreign language. ", "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Doctorate", "cdProgramWebsite": "theatre.umn.edu", "feV9J": "<p>Fall 2026: Rebuilt Program Requirement section into separate blocks; otherwise, no changes.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "120", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "120", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMax": "56", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "51", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Urban Studies is an interdisciplinary major that prepares students for careers in urban planning, public policy, and the nonprofit sector. The program offers students a mix of academic, hands-on, experiential, and skill-based coursework anchored by one of four themes: urban social/cultural issues, urban political economy and planning, urban infrastructure and environment, or international urban issues. The course mix includes foundational courses on urbanization, a wide range of electives that allow students to tailor their degree program, and a diverse mix of workshops taught by local urban professionals in topics such as real estate development, transit planning, and community engagement. Students who major in Urban Studies come to understand the way cities work and use the vast resources of the Twin Cities to explore their interests. All students complete one required 120-hour internship as part of their exploration of careers they might pursue. Finding an internship is great practice for a job hunt. With the vast local network of alumni of the program, there are many internship possibilities each term.

The program offers one-on-one advising, and students come to know one another through shared classes and through the Minnesota Urban Studies Student Association.

Urban Studies graduates are sought after in local and regional government agencies and community-based organizations and are highly competitive for graduate programs in fields such as social work, law, urban planning, finance, and geography.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": true, "cdProgramDualDegCredInCommonMax": 37, "cdProgramDualDegMemo": [{"bucketName": "coursedog-static-public", "name": "URBS_MURP_idp_mou_6_27_24 (signed) FINAL.pdf", "path": "files/form/1729178999465/u1NJKqUop46e.pdf"}], "cdProgramDualDegTitle": "Integrated Urban Studies BS / Master of Urban and Regional Planning", "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Social Sciences and Human Services", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>The Department of Geography, Environment, and Society is home to five majors and three minors, each of which is aligned with the Core Career Competency (CCC) framework from the CLA Career Readiness initiative. Each course in our department centers at least one of the ten CLA career competencies. The CCC framework is a CLA-specific framing of the University Student Learning Outcomes structure that facilitates a deepened student capacity for reflection and connection to their learning process and outcomes as they embark on their career trajectories.</p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsUndergrad": "8", "cdProgramNameShort": "Urban Stdy", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Baccalaureate", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cla.umn.edu/geography/undergraduate/majors-minors/urban-studies" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/geography/undergraduate/majors-minors/urban-studies</a></p>", "cdSamplePlanLink": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://sampleplan.umn.edu/node/1671">Urban Studies B.S.</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>fall 2026 catalog update</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Undergraduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "120", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "120", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMax": "44", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "36", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Urban Studies is an interdisciplinary major that prepares students for careers in urban planning, public policy, and the nonprofit sector. The program offers students a mix of academic, hands-on, experiential, and skill-based coursework anchored by one of four themes: urban social/cultural issues, urban political economy and planning, urban infrastructure and environment, or international urban issues. The course mix includes foundational courses on urbanization, a wide range of electives that allow students to tailor their degree program, and a diverse mix of workshops taught by local urban professionals in topics such as real estate development, transit planning, and community engagement. Students who major in urban studies come to understand the way cities work and use the vast resources of the Twin Cities to explore their interests. All students complete one required 120-hour internship as part of their exploration of careers they might pursue. Finding an internship is great practice for a job hunt. With the vast local network of alumni of the program, there are many internship possibilities each term.

The program offers one-on-one advising and students come to know one another through shared classes and through the Minnesota Urban Studies Student Association.

Urban Studies graduates are sought after in local and regional government agencies and community-based organizations and are highly competitive for graduate programs in fields such as social work, law, urban planning, finance, and geography. ", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": true, "cdProgramDualDegCredInCommonMax": 37, "cdProgramDualDegMemo": [{"bucketName": "coursedog-static-public", "name": "URBS_MURP_idp_mou_6_27_24 (signed) FINAL.pdf", "path": "files/form/1729178937455/rH96b4f3QKON.pdf"}], "cdProgramDualDegTitle": "Integrated Urban Studies BA / Master of Urban and Regional Planning", "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Social Sciences and Human Services", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>The Department of Geography, Environment, and Society is home to five majors and three minors, each of which is aligned with the Core Career Competency (CCC) framework from the CLA Career Readiness initiative. Each course in our department centers at least one of the ten CLA career competencies. The CCC framework is a CLA-specific framing of the University Student Learning Outcomes structure that facilitates a deepened student capacity for reflection and connection to their learning process and outcomes as they embark on their career trajectories.</p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsUndergrad": "8", "cdProgramNameShort": "Urban Stdy", "cdProgramReqLanguageSemesters": "4", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "Any second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Baccalaureate", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cla.umn.edu/geography/undergraduate/majors-minors/urban-studies" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/geography/undergraduate/majors-minors/urban-studies</a></p>", "cdSamplePlanLink": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://sampleplan.umn.edu/node/621">Urban Studies B.A.</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>fall 2026 catalog update</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Undergraduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCOA - College of Food, Agricultural and Natural Resource Sciences", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": "3", "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": false, "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": false, "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCollegeApprover": "TCOA - College of Food, Agricultural and Natural Resource Sciences", "cdProgramCreditsDoctMinorMax": 12, "cdProgramCreditsDoctMinorMin": "12", "cdProgramCreditsMastMinorMax": 9, "cdProgramCreditsMastMinorMin": "9", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDeliverySite": "University of Minnesota Duluth", "cdProgramDescr": "This cross-campus interdisciplinary program provides comprehensive training in water resources science, with integration across scientific disciplines. The minor benefits from a set of core courses plus electives in the following areas of interest: aquatic biology, environmental chemistry, hydrologic science, limnology, water management technology, water policy, water quality, and watershed science and management. 

The goal of the WRS program is to produce scientists with strong technical skills in disciplines relevant to water resources and a broad understanding of 1) the hydrologic cycle and associated ecosystems, 2) the interconnectedness of the sciences involved in managing aquatic resources, and 3) the interplay between the biophysical sciences and social sciences in developing and implementing public policies related to water.

Students in the program develop the breadth of scientific knowledge appropriate to understand the complicated aquatic ecosystems and watersheds on which they will work, as well as social dimensions of the topic, including the public policy and legal frameworks in which water resources are protected and managed.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": true, "cdProgramDualDegCredInCommonMax": 0, "cdProgramDualDegMemo": [{"bucketName": "coursedog-static-public", "name": "WRS MOU January 2024 ALL signed.docx", "path": "files/form/1705431785975/l7Ab46HKayj1.docx"}], "cdProgramDualDegTitle": "N/A - updated program MOU only", "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Math, Engineering, and Science", "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": 3, "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradMinorLevel": "Both Masters and Doctoral", "cdProgramLOs": "Students will graduate with strong technical skills in disciplines relevant to water resources and a broad understanding of 1) the hydrologic cycle and associated ecosystems, 2) the interconnectedness of the sciences involved in managing aquatic resources, and 3) the interplay between the biophysical sciences and social sciences in developing and implementing public policies related to water.", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "8", "cdProgramMinorType": "Minor related to a major", "cdProgramNameShort": "WaterResSc", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": true, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Graduate minor", "cdProgramWebsite": "wrs.umn.edu", "feV9J": "<p>No current changes. </p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "reportAsNscProgram": false, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCOA - College of Food, Agricultural and Natural Resource Sciences", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "120", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "120", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMax": "88", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "84", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Businesses, governments, and nonprofit organizations are making decisions with sustainability in mind with increasing frequency. They recognize that to ensure positive environmental, economic, and social outcomes, they must prepare comprehensive, long-term plans and employ informed, proficient individuals to carry them out. To accomplish these tasks, these public and private entities rely upon the expertise of employees and consultants who possess both a broad understanding of sustainability and an in-depth familiarity with the particular sector in which they operate.

The sustainable systems management major prepares students to enter the workforce with the knowledge and skills necessary to design, assess, implement, and manage systems to advance the goals of sustainability. Key features of this inherently interdisciplinary major include its skills-based and content-based integrated core courses, its emphasis on the development of strong analytical and quantitative skill sets, and its broad requirement of foundation courses in mathematics, science, business, economics, history, and policy. 

Students choose to specialize in one of four tracks: (1) corporate sustainability systems, (2) sustainable products business management, (3) building science and technology, or (4) energy systems.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Business", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>-Students can identify, define, and solve problems.</p><p>-Students can locate and critically evaluate information.</p><p>-Students have mastered a body of knowledge and a mode of inquiry.</p><p>-Students understand diverse philosophies and culture within and across societies.</p><p>-Students can communicate effectively.</p><p>-Students understand the role of creativity, innovation, discovery, and expression across disciplines.</p><p>-Students have acquired skills for effective citizenship and lifelong learning.</p><p></p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsUndergrad": "8", "cdProgramNameShort": "SusSysMgmt", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": true, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Baccalaureate", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://ssm.umn.edu/">http://ssm.umn.edu</a></p>", "cdSamplePlanLink": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://sampleplan.umn.edu/node/296">Sustainable Systems Management B.S.</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>Added university student learning outcomes to program description </p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Undergraduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCED - College of Education and Human Development", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": true, "cdProgramAdmissionCreditMin": "45", "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "120", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "120", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": 60, "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The sport management major prepares students to identify, define, and solve problems in the management, development, and delivery of sport in local and global contexts. Program coursework will challenge students to examine sport from a variety of perspectives, including social, psychological, legal, and economic. Graduates are prepared to think innovatively about the manner by which sport impacts, and is impacted by, broader cultural, economic, technological, and societal shifts. 

Graduates may find employment in sport marketing and management, parks and recreation, agency and advertising, sales and fundraising, community sport, coaching, community outreach and engagement, event and facilities management, and other sport, physical activity, or fitness-related occupations. The program also prepares students for graduate study in sport management and broader fields.

Coursework in sport management addresses topics such as legal issues and risk management, career exploration, ethics and sport, sport as a sociocultural phenomenon, corporate social responsibility in sport, organizational behavior, organizational theory, sport business, sport marketing, sport entrepreneurship, and facility and event management. A hallmark of the program is its strong connection with the Minnesota sports community. As a result, students have wide-ranging opportunities to gain hands-on experience in the sports industry throughout their time in the program.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Education", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>This program supports the University of Minnesota Student Learning Outcomes through courses required for the major.  <br><br><em>Students pursuing this major will take coursework that support the development of their proficiency in:<br></em></p><p>Possessing a sound understanding of the sport industry, including its structure, key industry roles and players, current trends, and challenges/opportunities.<br></p><p>Possessing strong practical management skills, with the capability to lead teams, make strategic decisions, and manage responsibilities and change in a fast-paced, evolving environment.<br></p><p>Demonstrating excellent written and verbal communication skills to work effectively in a team or sport organization, including communicating with diverse stakeholders such as athletes, sponsors, media, fans, donors, and administrators.<br></p><p>Engaging in a proactive,&nbsp;solution-oriented mindset, identifying challenges, analyzing problems, and implementing evidence-based solutions.<br></p><p>Demonstrating strong networking skills to build and maintain relationships and foster partnerships and opportunities with key actors within the sport industry.<br></p><p>Demonstrating proficiency in data analysis by making informed decisions based on key performance indicators, research data, market trends, and other relevant data.<br></p><p>Using an entrepreneurial mindset to develop innovative strategies for marketing, event management, and business operations in the sports industry.</p><p></p><p>Demonstrating a commitment to ethical conduct and professionalism, understanding the importance of integrity in sport management.<br></p><p>Demonstrating a strong commitment to using sport to drive social impact and for stakeholders to be socially responsible.</p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsUndergrad": "8", "cdProgramNameShort": "Sport Mgmt", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Baccalaureate", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><strong>Advising</strong><br><strong>High school students</strong><br><a href="https://admissions.tc.umn.edu/apply" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Contact the University Admissions Office</a><br>&nbsp;<br><strong>Transfer students</strong><br><a href="https://www.cehd.umn.edu/undergraduate/transfer" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">CEHD Transfer Admissions</a><br><a href="mailto:cehdtrfr@umn.edu" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">cehdtrfr@umn.edu</a><br>&nbsp;<br><strong>University of Minnesota students</strong><br><a href="https://www.cehd.umn.edu/undergraduate/transfer/internal" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Application to transfer</a>&nbsp;from within the U of M system<br><a href="mailto:cehdtrfr@umn.edu" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">cehdtrfr@umn.edu</a><br>&nbsp;<br><strong>CEHD students</strong><br>Call or email CEHD Student Services to schedule an appointment with your student services advisor.<br>Phone:&nbsp;612-625-3339<br>Email:&nbsp;<a href="mailto:cehdinfo@umn.edu" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">cehdinfo@umn.edu</a><br>&nbsp;<br><strong>Sport Management BS majors</strong><br>School of Kinesiology<br>Phone: 612-625-5300 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Fax: 612-626-7700<br>Email: <a href="mailto:kin@umn.edu" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">kin@umn.edu</a></p>", "cdSamplePlanLink": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://sampleplan.umn.edu/node/216">Sport Management B.S.</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>2026-2028 Catalog Update 1/13/26 MSK</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Undergraduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCED - College of Education and Human Development", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": true, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseMin": "1", "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": true, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmitMin": "2.75", "cdProgramAdmissionGPARationale": "Admission preference is given to students who have completed liberal education requirements and have an overall GPA of 2.00 before the admission deadline. Because of a large number of applicants, a 2.75 GPA is recommended.", "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": true, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": true, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUTMin": "2.75", "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransMin": "2.75", "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "17", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Sport Management is the business side of the sport industry that blends the unique aspects of sport with common business, marketing, management, and social and ethical principles.

The Sport Management minor helps prepare students for employment in the sport, entertainment, and/or fitness industries. This program allows students from any major at the University to obtain a minor in Sport Management.

Students who complete this minor may work in fields such as professional, semi-professional, collegiate, high school, and youth sport; facilities and event management; sport marketing and sales; communications; sporting goods manufacturing management; sporting goods retail management; non-profit sports; private clubs; fitness industry management; and extreme sports.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramLOs": "<p>This program supports the University of Minnesota Student Learning Outcomes through courses required for the major.  <br><br><em>Students pursuing this minor will take coursework that support the development of their proficiency in:</em></p><p><em>-Mastering a body of knowledge and a mode of inquiry</em></p><p><em>-Locating and critically evaluating information</em></p><p><em>-Communicating effectively</em></p><p><em>-Identifying, defining and solving problems,</em></p><p><em>-Understanding diverse philosophies and cultures</em></p><p><em>-Understanding the role of creativity, innovation, discovery and expression</em></p><p><em>-Acquiring skills for effective citizenship and lifelong learning.</em></p>", "cdProgramMinorType": "Minor related to a major", "cdProgramNameShort": "Sport Mgmt", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Undergraduate minor", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><strong>Advising</strong><br><strong>High school students</strong><br><a href="https://admissions.tc.umn.edu/apply" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Contact the University Admissions Office</a><br>&nbsp;<br><strong>Transfer students</strong><br><a href="https://www.cehd.umn.edu/undergraduate/transfer" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">CEHD Transfer Admissions</a><br><a href="mailto:cehdtrfr@umn.edu" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">cehdtrfr@umn.edu</a><br>&nbsp;<br><strong>University of Minnesota students</strong><br><a href="https://www.cehd.umn.edu/undergraduate/transfer/internal" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Application to transfer</a>&nbsp;from within the U of M system<br><a href="mailto:cehdtrfr@umn.edu" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">cehdtrfr@umn.edu</a><br>&nbsp;<br><strong>CEHD students</strong><br>Call or email CEHD Student Services to schedule an appointment with your student services advisor.<br>Phone:&nbsp;612-625-3339<br>Email:&nbsp;<a href="mailto:cehdinfo@umn.edu" target="_blank" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">cehdinfo@umn.edu</a><br>&nbsp;<br><strong>Sport Management minors</strong><br>School of Kinesiology<br>Phone: 612-625-5300 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Fax: 612-626-7700<br>Email: <a href="mailto:kin@umn.edu" class="open_in_new_tab" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">kin@umn.edu</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>Catalog Review 2026-2028. Adding SMGT 3634, 4520 to electives</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "reportAsNscProgram": false, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "120", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "120", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "36", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Global Studies students use a wide-angle lens to cultivate broad perspectives on the contemporary world. For example, you will learn to think of climate change not only in terms of CO2 levels in the atmosphere, but also in terms of politics, ecologies, social movements, and global inequalities. When you examine migration, you will study not only laws and non-governmental organizations' analyses of mobility trends, but the geopolitical upheavals and food systems failures that drive these trends. Students will always keep in view the grassroots and community initiatives that address these problems on the ground, since that is where the impacts of these issues are most powerfully felt and creative solutions worked out. In short, Global Studies is a major for students who are committed both to understanding the world they live in and to making that world a better place. 

Global Studies courses traverse geography and time. Some analyze the workings of global institutions and explore the operations of the global health, food, and financial systems. Others use works of art and philosophy from around the world to expose students to cultural forms like indigenous art and prison literature. Still others explore long term historical trajectories, such as the growth of global communications, media, and tourism industries. You will also have the opportunity to study abroad, intern, and engage deeply with on and off campus communities. In capstone projects, you will bring together what you've studied to define, explore, and create new visions of the world. You will learn both critical thinking and practice the empathy needed to construct global citizenship for the 21st century.

All learning experiences offer ample opportunities for developing the communications and organizational skills that support success in future careers in any number of sectors including nonprofits, government, and industry, as well as for graduate study in a number of arenas in law, policy, social sciences, and the humanities.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Communications, Languages, and Cultural Studies", "cdProgramLOs": "<p>We use approved program specific learning outcomes:</p><p>Students will have the ability to think theoretically and across disciplines about questions regarding globalization and culture, representation and power, and epistemology/knowledge making.</p><p>Students will have the ability to think theoretically and across disciplines about questions regarding political, economic, spatial and historical moorings of global modernity, and its filial formations, the nation-state and capitalism</p><p>Students will know how to ask a research question, conduct relevant primary and secondary source research, and synthesize knowledge to suggest a new argument or analysis.</p><p>Students will know how to communicate effectively and in appropriate genre and media.</p><p>Students will learn how to think critically about categories, institutions, and systems of power.</p><p>Students will explore their positionality in a globalized world and the ethics of their choices.</p>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsUndergrad": "8", "cdProgramNameShort": "Global Stu", "cdProgramReqLanguageSemesters": "4", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "Any second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Baccalaureate", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cla.umn.edu/global-studies" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/global-studies</a></p>", "cdSamplePlanLink": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://sampleplan.umn.edu/node/476">Global Studies B.A.</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p data-indent="1">course inactivation, removing it.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Undergraduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "15", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Global Studies students use a wide-angle lens to cultivate broad perspectives on the contemporary world. For example, you will learn to think of climate change not only in terms of CO2 levels in the atmosphere, but also in terms of politics, ecologies, social movements, and global inequalities. When you examine migration, not only will you study laws and non-governmental organizations' analyses of mobility trends, but also the geopolitical upheavals and food systems failures that drive these trends. Students will always keep in view the grassroots and community initiatives that address these problems on the ground, since that is where the impacts of these issues are most powerfully felt and creative solutions are worked out. In short, Global Studies is a minor for students who are committed both to understanding the world they live in and to making that world a better place.

Global Studies courses traverse geography and time. Some analyze the workings of global institutions and explore the operations of the global health, food, and financial systems. Others use works of art and philosophy from around the world to expose students to cultural forms like indigenous art and prison literature. Still others explore long-term historical trajectories, such as the growth of global communications, media, and tourism industries. You will also have the opportunity to study abroad, intern, and engage deeply with on and off-campus communities. You will learn both critical thinking and practice the empathy needed to construct global citizenship for the 21st century.

All learning experiences offer ample opportunities for developing the communications and organizational skills that support success in future careers in any number of sectors, including nonprofits, government, and industry, as well as for graduate study in a number of arenas in law, policy, social sciences, and the humanities.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramLOs": "<p>We use approved program specific learning outcomes:</p><p>Students will have the ability to think theoretically and across disciplines about questions regarding globalization and culture, representation and power, and epistemology/knowledge making.</p><p>Students will have the ability to think theoretically and across disciplines about questions regarding political, economic, spatial and historical moorings of global modernity, and its filial formations, the nation-state and capitalism</p><p>Students will know how to ask a research question, conduct relevant primary and secondary source research, and synthesize knowledge to suggest a new argument or analysis.</p><p>Students will know how to communicate effectively and in appropriate genre and media.</p><p>Students will learn how to think critically about categories, institutions, and systems of power.</p><p>Students will explore their positionality in a globalized world and the ethics of their choices.</p>", "cdProgramMinorType": "Minor related to a major", "cdProgramNameShort": "Global Stu", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "No second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Undergraduate minor", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cla.umn.edu/global-studies" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/global-studies</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>course inactivation, removing the course.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "reportAsNscProgram": false, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "Test of English as a Foreign language (TOEFL)", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": false, "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "Students interested in the minor are strongly encouraged to confer with their major field advisor and director of graduate studies, and the Technical Communication director of graduate studies, regarding feasibility and requirements.

Research master's and PhD students interested in rhetorical theory and history, technical communication, technology and culture, digital and new media studies, and writing pedagogy are encouraged to pursue the Rhetoric, Scientific and Technical Communication minor.

For an online application or for more information about graduate education admissions, see the General Information section of this website.", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": false, "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsMastMinorMax": 6, "cdProgramCreditsMastMinorMin": 6, "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["Completely Online"], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "Completely Online", "cdProgramDescr": "The Technical Communication minor is available for master-level students enrolled in other University graduate programs. Courses train students to apply basic theory and research-driven approaches to create and adapt content to solve complex problems in technical communication. The minor offers online courses in such areas as editing and style, writing with digital technologies, information design, and international professional communication. Coursework emphasizes collaboration with workplace professionals through client projects, virtual and global teamwork, mentorships, and emerging technologies, and enables students to develop unique strengths in digital, usability, and/or science/health/medical communication.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": 2.8, "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradMinorLevel": "Masters", "cdProgramLOs": "In the Graduate Minor in Technical Communication program at the University of Minnesota, students apply research-driven approaches to create and adapt content to solve complex problems in technical communication workplaces. Students connect with workplace professionals through client projects, virtual and global teamwork, mentorships, and emerging technologies. These experiences enable students to develop unique strengths in digital, usability, and science/health/medical communication.

As part of these experience-driven programs, students do the following:
-Understand the history, professionalism, and discourse of the field, including the ability to apply rhetorical theory/principles to scientific and technical communication;
-Visualize, write, design, and manage content for multiple media and contexts;
-Lead local/virtual/global teams in the design, development, and evaluation of scientific and technical communication;
-Plan and conduct research to meet user and strategic work needs;
-Construct and foster connected, collaborative, interactive environments; and
-Apply technical communication research to a specific industry (e.g., health, technology, usability, public policy, education).

These learning outcomes were collaboratively developed by program leadership and instructors.
", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": 2, "cdProgramMinorType": "Minor related to a major", "cdProgramNewCompAdv": "No program changes other than name change", "cdProgramNewConsult": [{"bucketName": "coursedog-static-public", "name": "Copy of Writing Studies Proposal - Graduate Minor Name Change.pdf", "path": "files/form/1700157999928/bFRAKfGUqnZq.pdf"}], "cdProgramNewCourseCreditCount": 0, "cdProgramNewDEI": "No program changes other than name change", "cdProgramNewDemand": "No program changes other than name change", "cdProgramNewDev": "No program changes other than name change", "cdProgramNewInterrelated": "No program changes other than name change", "cdProgramNewMission": "No program changes other than name change", "cdProgramNewResources": "No program changes other than name change", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqOther": "A minimum grade of B- is required for all courses applied to the minor, unless the course is only offered S/N. The overall minimum GPA is 2.80.", "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Graduate minor", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p>https:///<a href="https://cla.umn.edu/writing-studies" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/writing-studies</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>Fall 2026: No updates.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "sJszy": false, "status": "Active"}

{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionCourseCreditMin": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionPreMajorStatus": "No", "cdProgramCareer": "Undergraduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": "120", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "120", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMax": "40", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "33", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "Studies in cinema and media culture (SCMC) examines cinema and media by emphasizing its location within the intricate social, historical, and cultural matrix of audiovisual forms and practices. Core courses and electives are offered not only in the Department of Cultural Studies and Comparative Literature (CSCL), but also in a number of other contributing departments. Through the program's interdisciplinary framework, students explore the sounds and images of cinema and media as they have changed throughout the 19th and 20th centuries. Print, photography, radio, television, video, and digital media are also considered crucial to understanding our media cultures. Students develop the ability to "read" the production and circulation of meaning in cinema and media, especially within the institutions of mass culture; examine the history of cinema and media cultures; engage the cross-cultural and global dynamics of production and reception; and explore the theoretical models that have shaped thinking about the cinema and media.

Although the major includes a production component, its principal focus is on cultural contexts, history, and theory.

For the latest information on the SCMC major, visit the CSCL website.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Communications, Languages, and Cultural Studies", "cdProgramLOs": "<ul><li><p>Students will master a body of knowledge and a mode of inquiry</p></li><li><p>Students will acquire skills for effective citizenship and lifelong learning</p></li><li><p>Students will understand the role of creativity, innovation, discovery, and expression across disciplines</p></li><li><p>Students will understand diverse philosophies and culture within and across societies</p></li><li><p>Students will be able to identify, define, and solve problems</p></li></ul>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsUndergrad": "8", "cdProgramNameShort": "Cinema,Med", "cdProgramReqLanguageSemesters": "4", "cdProgramReqLanguageUgrd": "Any second language", "cdProgramReqSubplan": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Baccalaureate", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://cla.umn.edu/cscl/undergraduate/majors-minors/studies-cinema-media-culture" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://cla.umn.edu/cscl/undergraduate/majors-minors/studies-cinema-media-culture</a></p>", "cdSamplePlanLink": "<p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="open_in_new_tab" href="https://sampleplan.umn.edu/node/606">Studies in Cinema and Media Culture B.A.</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>fall 2026 catalog update</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Undergraduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

