{"JBsh4": false, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TALA - College of Design", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": false, "cdProgramAdmissionDegreeGrad": "There are no preferred prior graduate or professional degrees, but the program compliments students currently pursuing graduate degrees in Architecture, Landscape Architecture, and Urban Planning.", "cdProgramAdmissionDegreeUgrd": "An undergraduate degree in architecture, landscape architecture, interior design, urban planning, civil engineering, or from another design, engineering, and/or built environment field is preferred.", "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": 3, "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSReadScore": 6.5, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSWritScore": 6.5, "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLReadScore": "19", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": "79", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLWritScore": "21", "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsProgramMin": "12", "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The metropolitan design certificate at the College of Design prepares students with the essential knowledge and understanding of the city within the complexities of the 21st-century metropolis.

The certificate is open to graduate students in the College of Design and other colleges. The certificate is a 12-credit program.

Interested students should enroll during the second semester (spring) of graduate studies. It is recommended that students make a decision to enroll in the certificate early so that the completion of courses can be made within the time required for completion of the professional degree.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": false, "cdProgramFieldofStudy": "Art, Design, and the Performing Arts", "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "2.800", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": false, "cdProgramLOs": "<p>In this program<em>, </em>students will analyze complex 21st-century metropolitan systems to develop integrative design solutions that address social, environmental, and infrastructural urban challenges.</p><p>The program utilizes the  following learning outcomes:</p><ul><li><p>Identify, define, and solve problems</p></li><li><p>Mastered a body of knowledge</p></li></ul>", "cdProgramLengthInTermsGrad": "2", "cdProgramMinorAllowed": false, "cdProgramNameShort": "Metro Dsgn", "cdProgramReqLanguageGrad": false, "cdProgramReqSubplanGrad": false, "cdProgramStatus": "Active", "cdProgramSubplanMoreThanOne": false, "cdProgramSumTerms": false, "cdProgramTypeManual": "Post-baccalaureate credit certificate/licensure/endorsement", "cdProgramWebsite": "<p><a href="https://design.umn.edu/academics/explore-all-certificates/metropolitan-design-graduate-certificate" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">https://design.umn.edu/academics/explore-all-certificates/metropolitan-design-graduate-certificate</a></p>", "feV9J": "<p>Catalog update - no curriculum changes were made.</p>", "g9JMc": false, "o8EfQ": false, "programLevelCareer": "Graduate", "reportAsNscProgram": true, "sJszy": false, "saaWhifDispAdvr": true, "saaWhifDispPrem": true, "saaWhifDispStd": true, "splitOwnership": false, "status": "Active"}

{"3cRbz": "Accredited.", "JBsh4": true, "JgOA0": false, "NK1s2": false, "Oin5M": ["Minnesota", "North Dakota", "Wisconsin"], "RF2TB": "Peter Mackenzie", "TmgiH": false, "UoHKm": false, "X1TcH": true, "YUCsS": "https://cla.umn.edu/slhs/graduate/degrees/aud-audiology", "a3nTH": false, "cdDegreeGrantingCollege": "TCLA - College of Liberal Arts", "cdProgramAcadExchange": false, "cdProgramAccredited": true, "cdProgramAccreditedName": "Council on Academic Accreditation in Audiology and Speech-Language Pathology (CAA).", "cdProgramAccreditedNextRev": "2027-01-01", "cdProgramAccreditedRevFreq": "Every 8 years", "cdProgramAccreditedWebsite": "https://caa.asha.org/", "cdProgramAdmissionEngTestPref": "No Preferred Test", "cdProgramAdmissionGMAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPACollAdmit": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATrans": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPATransIUT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionGPAUgrdMin": "2.8", "cdProgramAdmissionGRE": false, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTS": true, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSReadScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSTotScore": 7, "cdProgramAdmissionIELTSWritScore": "6.5", "cdProgramAdmissionLSAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionMCAT": false, "cdProgramAdmissionOthAppReqs": "Students with any academic background may apply and can be accepted. However, students need to complete the below prerequisites or their equivalents at other institutions before taking graduate level courses. If a student did not take all these courses as an undergraduate, they must take them at the start of their AuD program.

- Statistics - 1 full semester course required with hypothesis testing
- Anatomy and Physiology of Speech and Hearing - 1 full semester course required
- Hearing Science - 1 full semester course required
- Previous Coursework in social/behavioral sciences, biological sciences, and physical sciences (physics or chemistry) is recommended

All offers of AuD admissions are contingent upon the results of a criminal background check administered by the Minnesota Department of Human Services and completion of immunization requirements.
", "cdProgramAdmissionOtherTest": false, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFL": true, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLReadScore": "19", "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLTotScore": 100, "cdProgramAdmissionTOEFLWritScore": "21", "cdProgramCareer": "Graduate", "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMax": 102, "cdProgramCreditsDegreeMin": "102", "cdProgramCreditsDoctMajMax": 102, "cdProgramCreditsDoctMajMin": 102, "cdProgramCreditsDoctThesis": 0, "cdProgramDeliveryAddl": ["In-person/classroom"], "cdProgramDeliveryAddlHybridType": [], "cdProgramDeliveryPrimary": "In-person/classroom", "cdProgramDescr": "The purpose of the AuD program is to prepare clinical practitioners in the field of audiology with an interest in hearing and balance disorders. Audiology students will be taught to implement evidence-based practice with their clients/patients. Students must demonstrate the ability to interpret and apply current and future findings in the field and must have excellent analytical and communication skills. AuD program students will receive a combination of intensive academic and clinical education from coursework, laboratory experiences, and patient/client contact. The AuD program also ensures that graduates are eligible for state and national credentials in audiology.

Individuals interested in pursuing an advanced degree in audiology should apply directly to the audiology AuD program. Students admitted to the AuD are eligible to apply to the University's Speech-Language-Hearing Sciences MA/Audiology track.

The Doctor of Audiology (Au.D.) education program in audiology {residential} at the University of Minnesota, Twin Cities is accredited by the Council on Academic Accreditation in Audiology and Speech-Language Pathology of the American Speech-Language-Hearing Association, 2200 Research Boulevard, #310, Rockville, MD 20850, 800-498-2071 or 301-296-5700.", "cdProgramDisplayCatalog": true, "cdProgramDualDeg": true, "cdProgramDualDegCredInCommonMax": "9", "cdProgramDualDegMemo": [{"bucketName": "coursedog-static-public", "name": "AuD in Audiology Coursedog Revisions - Requirements Section.pdf", "path": "files/form/1701448237147/wG7Oc9O76ssI.pdf"}], "cdProgramDualDegTitle": "AuD/PhD in Speech-Language-Hearing Sciences", "cdProgramGPAGoodStanding": "3.000", "cdProgramGradMinor4xxx": true, "cdProgramGradResGradProf": "Graduate Professional", "cdProgramLOs": "In all programs, we strive to serve scholars within the University and broader community with interwoven coursework and clinical and research experiences. These enable students to:
    -understand the complexities of human communication across the life span;
   - implement evidence-based assessment and treatment strategies;
    -operate in a manner consistent with state and national-level codes of ethics;
